\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 1\\}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\n\\]\n\nso the number of points is \\(n+1-b\\le n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}= \\{(a,c-a)\\mid \\max\\{1,c-n\\}\\le a\\le \\min\\{n,c-1\\}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Counting Argument  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.  \nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\n2k+n(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4),\n\n\\[\n(2n-4)k\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) the coefficient \\(2n-4\\) is positive, so dividing (5) by it yields\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2n-4}\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}<1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the only integer satisfying (6) is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines. The lines are distinct and none of them is sunny. Thus a covering with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nThe counting in