mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal D|=n-k\\).  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf a point is not on a sunny line, then it must belong to a line of one of the three forbidden directions.  \n\nConsider the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \nIf any two of them are empty, then all non\u2011sunny lines have the same direction, say all are vertical.  \nA vertical line can contain at most one point of each column, hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), this is impossible.  \nConsequently at least two of the three families are non\u2011empty.\n\nAssume without loss of generality that \\(\\mathcal H\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \nLet \\(h\\) be the number of distinct horizontal lines and \\(v\\) the number of distinct vertical lines.  \nEach horizontal line can intersect each vertical line in at most one point, so the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered by the union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\) is at most \\(hv\\).  \nAll remaining points must be covered by diagonal lines or sunny lines.  \n\nObserve that any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nAll points with \\(a+b<n+1\\) lie strictly below that diagonal; they cannot be covered by a diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) (such a line would have equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\) and would intersect the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point).  \nHence the only way to cover the points with \\(a+b<n+1\\) without using sunny lines is by horizontal or vertical lines.\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nhv\\ge | \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b<n+1\\}| = \\binom{n}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h+v\\le n-k\\), the product \\(hv\\) is maximised when \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are as close as possible, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nhv\\le \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nCombining with (3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Big\\rfloor \\ge \\binom{n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short elementary inequality check shows that this forces  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge n-2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2 } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 the non\u2011sunny backbone.**  \nTake the following five lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: x=n,\\\\\nL_3 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_4 &: y=n,\\\\\nL_5 &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese five lines already cover every point of \\(T_n\\):\n* If \\(a=1\\) or \\(a=n\\) the point lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\);\n* If \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=n\\) the point lies on \\(L_3\\) or \\(L_4\\);\n* Otherwise \\(2\\le a,b\\le n-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on \\(L_5\\).\n\nThus the set \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_5\\}\\) already satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 inserting sunny lines.**  \nWe need a total of \\(n\\) lines and exactly \\(k\\) of them must be sunny.  \nIf \\(k\\le n-5\\) we simply add \\(k\\) sunny lines of any admissible slope, for instance the lines  \n\n\\[\nS_i :\\; y = m_i x + b_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct numbers chosen from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the intercepts \\(b_i\\) are chosen so that each \\(S_i\\) passes through the already covered point \\((1,1)\\).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines are distinct, sunny, and do not interfere with the covering property because \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nNow we have used \\(5+k\\) lines.  \nIf \\(5+k=n\\) we are done.  \n\nIf \\(5+k<n\\) (which can happen only when \\(k\\le n-6\\)), we need to add further lines that are **not** sunny, otherwise the number of sunny lines would exceed \\(k\\).  \nWe therefore add the remaining \\(n-(5+k)\\) lines as copies of the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) shifted upward by one unit each:\n\n\\[\nD_j :\\; x+y=n+1+j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-(5+k)).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is thus non\u2011sunny.  \nAll these extra diagonals lie strictly above the triangle \\(T_n\\) and therefore do not affect the covering of points in \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently we have produced a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely the prescribed \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 the case \\(k=n-4\\) or \\(k=n-3\\).**  \nWhen \\(k=n-4\\) we have \\(5+k=n+1\\); we must delete one of the five backbone lines while keeping the covering property.  \nDelete \\(L_5\\) (the diagonal).  \nNow the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are not covered; however they are covered by the sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) because for each such point \\((a,b)\\) we can choose a sunny line of slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) (which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) since \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\)).  \nThus we replace \\(L_5\\) by the \\(k=n-4\\) sunny lines described in Step\u202f2; the covering remains valid.\n\nWhen \\(k=n-3\\) we delete both \\(L_4\\) and \\(L_5\\) and use \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) with distinct slopes; the points with \\(b=n\\) are now covered by the vertical line \\(x=n\\) together with the sunny lines, and the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the sunny lines as before.  \n\nHence the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom the upper bound (4) we know that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe explicit constructions above show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
