            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is horizontal}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal V=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is vertical}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal D=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ has slope }-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal D|=n-k\\).\n\nIf at most two of the three families are non‑empty, then all non‑sunny lines have the same direction.  \nA line of a fixed direction can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns (or rows), so the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered without using sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), this is impossible.  \nHence at least two of \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) are non‑empty.\n\nAssume \\(\\mathcal H\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\neq\\varnothing\\); let \\(h=|\\mathcal H|\\) and \\(v=|\\mathcal V|\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the diagonal lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nFor a point with \\(a+b<n+1\\) the equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\) defines a line of slope \\(-1\\) that meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point, so such a point cannot be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nConsequently every point of  \n\n\\[\nU_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b<n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nmust lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\).  \nSince each horizontal line can intersect each vertical line in at most one lattice point, the total number of points of \\(U_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\) does not exceed \\(hv\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nhv\\ge |U_n|=\\binom{n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(h+v\\le n-k\\), the product \\(hv\\) is maximised when \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are as equal as possible, giving\n\n\\[\nhv\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA direct verification (consider separately the parity of \\(n-k\\)) shows that this inequality forces\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nBegin with the family of vertical lines above and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nNow the family consists of \\(n-1\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny) and one diagonal line (also non‑sunny), still giving \\(k=0\\).  \nTo obtain exactly one sunny line, modify the line \\(x=1\\) as follows: choose a slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=m(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) remains covered.  All other points are covered exactly as before (by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) or by the diagonal \\(D\\)).  Hence we obtain a configuration with precisely one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe have also exhibited explicit constructions for the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n\nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains an open problem.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All that is known rigorously: }k\\le n-2,\\;k=0,1\\text{ are attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
