            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The required point set  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nCounting the points:\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the integer form  \n\n\\[\nqx-py=c,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\), the vector \\((a'-a,b'-b)\\) is an integer multiple of \\((p,q)\\). Hence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p|+|q|\\) when we move from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Starting from the smallest possible value of \\(a+b\\) on \\(\\ell\\), we can increase this sum at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/(|p|+|q|)\\bigr\\rfloor\\) times before exceeding the bound \\(n+1\\). Consequently at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/(|p|+|q|)\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(\\ell\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), giving the final inequality. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal.*  For \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line contains \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.*  By symmetry the same bound holds.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  Such a line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe admissible integers \\(a\\) satisfy \\(\\max\\{1,c-n\\}\\le a\\le\\min\\{n,c-1\\}\\); the number of such integers is \\(\\min\\{c-1,2n+2-c\\}\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary counting inequality\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the sum of these maximal contributions cannot be smaller than \\(|T_n|\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (2):\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\bigr)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}\n      =\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\) is negative, dividing by it reverses the inequality:\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{\\,n/2\\,}{\\,n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow treat the two parity cases.\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).*  \n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\). Formula (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{m}{\\,2m-m-1\\,}=\\frac{m}{m-1}=1+\\frac{1}{m-1}\\le2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le2\\).\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).*  \n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{(2m+1)/2}{\\,2m+1-m-1\\,}\n   =\\frac{2m+1}{2m}=1+\\frac{1}{2m}<2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence for odd \\(n\\) we obtain \\(k\\le1\\).\n\nConsequently any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2\\}&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[1mm]\n\\{0,1\\}&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness of the bounds\n\nWe now exhibit configurations attaining every value allowed by (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence the union of the \\(n\\) lines is \\(T_n\\). All lines are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and \\(k=0\\) is always realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n\\): a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n\nLet \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).  \nBegin with the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except those with \\(b=n\\); the only such point is \\((1,n)\\).  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\) (already covered) and also the point \\((1,n)\\) because \\(n=2m+1\\) and \\(1+n=2m+2\\) is even; solving \\(y=x\\) with \\(y=n\\) gives \\(x=n\\), which is not in \\(T_n\\). Instead we observe that the missing point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny; therefore we keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and drop one horizontal line, say \\(y=n\\).  \n\nA concrete family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=x\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct; exactly one of them (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny. A direct check shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\ge2\\) (hence lies on \\(y=b\\)), or has \\(b=1\\) and then \\(a=1\\) (lies on \\(x=1\\)) or \\(a=b\\) (lies on \\(y=x\\)). Thus the configuration covers \\(T_n\\) with precisely one sunny line. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Even \\(n\\): configurations with one and with two sunny lines\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) (even, \\(m\\ge2\\)).\n\n*Two sunny lines.*  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\underbrace{\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=-x+2m+1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\]\n\nThe horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=1\\) and the point \\((1,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n\\), hence lies on \\(y=x\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(y=-x+2m+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{2m}\\) belongs to one of the listed lines. The family contains exactly two sunny lines, proving that \\(k=2\\) is realizable for even \\(n\\).\n\n*One sunny line.*  \nDelete the line \\(y=-x+2m+1\\) from the previous construction and add the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\). The resulting family has \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny, and still covers all points of \\(T_{2m}\\) (the points that were previously covered by \\(y=-x+2m+1\\) are now covered by the added horizontal line). Hence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument (inequality (2)) shows that no configuration can have more sunny lines than stated in (7). The explicit families constructed in Section 4 achieve every value allowed by (7). Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
