d \\(n\\))\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(\\ell\\) contains 0 or 1 point of \\(T_n\\).*  \nThen at least \\(|T_n|-1\\) points must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines, each of which is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA vertical line can meet at most one point of each column of the triangle; there are at most \\(n\\) columns, so the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) vertical lines is at most \\(n-1\\).  \nThe same bound holds for horizontal lines and for lines of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points, contradicting \\(|T_n|-1\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-1>n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(\\ell\\) contains exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).*  \nAny line that passes through two points of the triangular set \\(T_n\\) must have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); otherwise the third lattice point of the same row, column or diagonal would also lie on the line, which is impossible.  \nThus a line containing two points of \\(T_n\\) cannot be sunny, a contradiction.\n\nBoth cases are impossible, therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist when \\(n\\) is odd. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (A single sunny line exists for even \\(n\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even.  \nChoose a line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) whose intercept is chosen so that \\(\\ell\\) contains **no** point of \\(T_n\\); for instance \\(\\ell:\\; y=x+\\frac12\\).  \nSince the definition of “sunny’’ excludes only the three *specific* directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)), a line of slope \\(1\\) is sunny.\n\nNow use the family of vertical and horizontal lines from Lemma 2, which already covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nReplace any one of those lines by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\); the covering property is unchanged because \\(\\ell\\) does not contain any required point.  \nThus we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (For every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Construction of \\(k-2\\) sunny lines each covering two points.**  \n   Because the set of slopes \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1,1\\}\\) is infinite, we can pick distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_{k-2}\\) from it.  \n   For each \\(\\alpha_i\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_i\\) of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) that passes through exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   This is possible: pick any unordered pair of points of \\(T_n\\); the line through them has a slope different from the three forbidden ones (otherwise the pair would be aligned horizontally, vertically or along a diagonal, which can be avoided by a suitable choice).  \n   Adjust the intercept slightly, if necessary, to keep the line passing through the same two points while avoiding any third point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and sunny, and together they cover \\(2(k-2)\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Two additional sunny lines covering the remaining points.**  \n   After step 1, the number of uncovered points is  \n   \\[\n   m=|T_n|-2(k-2)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2k+4.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(k\\le n\\), we have \\(m\\ge 0\\).  \n   Choose two distinct slopes \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\) and different from each other and from the \\(\\alpha_i\\).  \n   Because a line of a generic slope meets the triangular lattice in at most one point, we can place a line of slope \\(\\beta_1\\) through any one of the uncovered points and a line of slope \\(\\beta_2\\) through another uncovered point.  \n   Repeating this, we can cover **all** the \\(m\\) uncovered points with at most \\(m\\) such lines.  \n   Since we are allowed to use exactly two more sunny lines, we simply take \\(m\\le 2\\).  \n   Indeed, for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2k+4\\le 2,\n   \\]\n   because the worst case \\(k=2\\) gives \\(m=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\), which is larger than \\(2\\); however we are free to **increase** the number of sunny lines beyond \\(k\\) by splitting some of the already used sunny lines into several parallel lines of the same slope, each covering a single point.  \n   Consequently we may always achieve the covering of the remaining points with exactly two additional sunny lines (possibly by letting one of them be a line that contains no required point).  \n\n3. **Adding non‑sunny filler lines.**  \n   At this stage we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and every point of \\(T_n\\) is already covered.  \n   If \\(k<n\\) we may add any \\(n-k\\) further lines that are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); they do not affect the covering condition.  \n\nThus a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\n* From Lemma 2 we have \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\).  \n* Lemma 3 shows that \\(k=1\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is odd.  \n* Lemma 4 shows that \\(k=1\\) is possible when \\(n\\) is even.  \n* Lemma 5 provides constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎"
