   diagonal point \\((a+h-(h),h)=(a,n+1-a)\\) (the construction in the next\n    proposition supplies such a line).  Hence the point remains covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is still contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  The family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) of Lemma 2 has no sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).  Apply Lemma 3 to any \\(k\\) of the horizontal\nlines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\); each application replaces one horizontal line by a\nsunny line while preserving the covering.  After \\(k\\) replacements we have a\nfamily with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nKeep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  For each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = x + (t-(n+1-t)) = x + (2t-n-1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the diagonal point\n\\((t,n+1-t)\\).  The \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) are distinct\n(since they have different intercepts) and all are sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,-1\\)).\nEach diagonal point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_t\\); thus the\nwhole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is covered.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Then \\((b,a)\\) is a point of the\ndiagonal, so it lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Because \\(\\ell_b\\) has equation\n\\(y=x+ (2b-n-1)\\), substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+(2b-n-1)=b\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\nby one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) together with the ve