nny, and \\(|\\mathcal F_{0}|=n\\).\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) and\n  contains the point.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\) then \\(a\\le2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n  – If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n  – If \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\); hence the point lies on the diagonal\n    line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nF_{0}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering family that contains the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(h\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le h\\le n-2\\) such that the horizontal line\n\\(y=h\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nChoose a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=\\alpha_h x+\\beta_h ,\\qquad\n\\alpha_h\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;\\beta_h\\notin\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{y=h\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points that were covered by lines other than \\(y=h\\) remain covered.  \nConsider a point \\((a,h)\\) that relied on the removed horizontal line.\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(a+h\\le n+1\\).\n\n  – When \\(a+h=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n  – When \\(a+h<n+1\\) the point \\((a,h)\\) is **not** on the diagonal, but it is\n    already covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a+h}\\) that passes through the\n    diagonal point \\((a+h-(h),h)=(a,n+1-a)\\) (the construction in the next\n    proposition supplies such a line).  Hence the point remains covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is still contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  The family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) of Lemma 2 has no sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).  Apply Lemma 3 to any \\(k\\) of the horizontal\nlines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\); each application replaces one horizontal line by a\nsunny line while preserving the covering.  After \\(k\\) replacements we have a\nfamily with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nKeep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  For each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = x + (t-(n+1-t)) = x + (2t-n-1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the diagonal point\n\\((t,n+1-t)\\).  The \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) are distinct\n(since they have different intercepts) and all are sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,-1\\)).\nEach diagonal point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_t\\); thus the\nwhole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is covered.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Then \\((b,a)\\) is a point of the\ndiagonal, so it lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Because \\(\\ell_b\\) has equation\n\\(y=x+ (2b-n-1)\\), substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a+(2b-n-1)=b\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\nby one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) together with the vertical\nline \\(x=1\\).  The family contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) a suitable family exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------