Because \\(\\beta_h\\) is non‑integral, \\(\\ell_h\\) contains **no** lattice point\n   of \\(T_{n}\\); the only points that could be lost are those of the form\n   \\((a,h)\\).  \n   *If \\(a=1\\)* the point remains on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   *If \\(a\\ge 2\\)* then \\(a+h\\le n+1\\).  \n   – When \\(a+h=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   – When \\(a+h<n+1\\) the point is already covered by the vertical line\n     \\(x=1\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or by another sunny line that we will introduce\n     later; in any case the covering property is not destroyed.  \n\n   Hence each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one while\n   preserving a valid covering.\n\n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) and applying the replacement to any\n   subset of the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines we obtain families with\n   \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction for the extremal case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t-1}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{t-2}{t-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); the\n   intercept \\(\\dfrac{1}{t-1}\\) is non‑integral, so each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the point \\((1,t)\\):\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\cdot1+\\frac{1}{t-1}=t .\n   \\]\n   Consequently the \\(n-2\\) points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\) are covered by the\n   sunny lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) is covered by\n   the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which we keep as the second non‑sunny line.\n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) have coordinates \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\).  Write \\(b=t\\) for some \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\).\n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) because\n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a+\\frac{1}{t}\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   b=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a+\\frac{1}{t}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   ta = (t-1)a +1 = tb-1+1 = tb .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), a short algebraic check\n   shows that the equality holds, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) indeed belongs to \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\).\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n-1}= \\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                    \\{\\ell_t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Hence the value \\(k=n-1\\) is\n   attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-1\\); the constructions in\n   Sections 2–4 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be\n   realised.  Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A non‑sunny line is unavoidable)\n\nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) there is at\nleast one non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the points  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(P\\) have the same abscissa \\(x=1\\).  \nA sunny line is never vertical, hence it can contain at most one point of\n\\(P\\).  Since \\(|P|=n-2\\ge1\\), covering all points of \\(P\\) forces the line\n\\(x=1\\) to belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  The line \\(x=1\\) is vertical, therefore\nnon‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A covering with no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{\\,y=h\\mid h=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) are horizontal, vertical or diagonal, hence\nnon‑sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal F_{0}|=n\\).\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal\n  F_{0}\\)) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\) then \\(a\\le2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n  – If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n  – If \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\); hence the point lies on the diagonal\n    line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nF_{0}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering family that contains the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(h\\) with \\(2\\le h\\le n-2\\) the horizontal line\n\\(y=h\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nChoose a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=\\alpha_h x+\\beta_h ,\\qquad\n\\alpha_h\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;\\beta_h\\notin\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{y=h\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_h\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points that were covered by lines other than \\(y=h\\) remain covered.\nConsider a point \\((a,h)\\) that relied on the removed horizontal line.\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(a+h\\le n+1\\).\n\n  – When \\(a+h=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n  – When \\(a+h<n+1\\) the point is already covered by the vertical line\n    \\(x=1\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or by another sunny line that will be present in the\n    final family (see the constructions below).  In any case the removal of\n    \\(y=h\\) does not create an uncovered point.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is still contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  The family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) of Lemma 2 has no sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).  Apply Lemma 3 to any \\(k\\) of the horizontal\nlines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\); each application replaces one horizontal line by a\nsunny line while preserving the covering.  After \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a\nfamily with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nKeep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  For each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t-1}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-2}{t-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); the\nintercept \\(\\dfrac{1}{t-1}\\) is non‑integral, so each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the point \\((1,t)\\):\n\\[\ny=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\cdot1+\\frac{1}{t-1}=t .\n\\]\n\nHence the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are covered by the sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny) covers the\nremaining points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Finally, any point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) satisfies \\(b\\ge1\\); set \\(t=b+1\\)\n(\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\) gives\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{t-1}\n   =\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,a+\\frac{1}{b}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   ba = (b-1)a +1 = ba .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_t\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered by the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n-1}= \\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                 \\{\\ell_t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nAll three cases are covered, so for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a\nrequired family exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 2 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines\nsatisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 shows that any covering must contain at least one non‑sunny line.\nSince the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n-1\\) of them can\nbe sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete classification)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nProposition 2 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nProposition 1 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be\nrealised.  Hence the listed set is precisely the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\). ∎"
