n-1,1)\\) one obtains the inequalities  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge 1,\\qquad h\\ge 1,\\qquad d\\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover the points \\((1,1),\\,(2,1),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\) are all on the diagonal family, forcing \\(d\\ge n-1\\); similarly the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\) force \\(v\\ge n-1\\).  \n   Adding these lower bounds gives  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+d\\ge 2(n-1) > n-1,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.  \n\nCombining the three parts yields the full classification of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); none is sunny.\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n-1,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n-1,\\qquad 2\\le a+b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies simultaneously on  \n\n\\[\nx=a\\in\\mathcal V,\\qquad y=b\\in\\mathcal H,\\qquad x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal D .\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least three of the above lines.\n\nWe need only \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Choose  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\in\\mathcal H\\;(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2),\\qquad\nL_{n-1}:x=1\\in\\mathcal V,\\qquad\nL_{n}:x+y=2\\in\\mathcal D .\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) is on the horizontal line \\(y=b=L_b\\).  \n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so the point is \\((1,n-1)\\) and lies on the vertical line \\(x=1=L_{n-1}\\).  \n* The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=2=L_n\\).\n\nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and none of the chosen lines is sunny.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is attainable for every }n\\ge 3. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones  \n\nWe prove that for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (One\u2011step replacement).**  \nLet a covering of \\(T_n\\) be given by a set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of sunny lines (pairwise non\u2011parallel) and a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines, with \\(|\\mathcal S|=t<n\\).  \nThen we can construct another covering in which the number of sunny lines is \\(t+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPick any line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\).  Three cases are possible.\n\n*Case A \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, \\(\\ell\\colon x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)).*  \nAll points of \\(\\ell\\) have the form \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-i\\).  \nEach such point also lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and on the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+b\\); both belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S\\) because we have not removed them.  \nConsequently, after deleting \\(\\ell\\) every point of \\(\\ell\\) remains covered **except possibly** the point \\((i,1)\\).  \n\nChoose a slope \\(m\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the already existing sunny lines (there are only finitely many forbidden slopes, so such an \\(m\\) exists).  \nLet \\(\\ell'\\) be the line of slope \\(m\\) passing through \\((i,1)\\).  \n\\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and is not parallel to any line already in \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nNow replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\n*Case B \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal.*  \nSymmetric to Case\u202fA (use the point \\((1,j)\\) on the line).\n\n*Case C \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal, \\(\\ell\\colon x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\).*  \nPoints on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\).  \nEach such point also lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and on the horizontal line \\(y=s-a\\).  \nDelete \\(\\ell\\) and insert a sunny line through the point \\((1,s-1)\\) with a slope avoiding the forbidden set and the slopes already present.  \nAll points of \\(\\ell\\) other than \\((1,s-1)\\) stay covered by the vertical or horizontal lines; the point \\((1,s-1)\\) is now covered by the new sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n**Iterating the replacement.**  \nStart from the configuration of part\u202f1, which has \\(t=0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2.1 repeatedly; after the \\(k\\)-th application we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, provided \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThus for every integer  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration satisfying the two requirements with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of a single sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly one sunny line \\(L\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines belong to the three forbidden families; denote by  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal C\\},\\qquad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal C\\},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal C\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 3.1.**  Each of the numbers \\(h,v,d\\) is at least \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\) belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \nAll these points have \\(y=1\\); none of them lies on the sunny line \\(L\\) because a sunny line cannot be horizontal.  \nTherefore each of them must be covered by a line of slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal).  \nIf a point \\((a,1)\\) were covered by a diagonal line, that line would be \\(x+y=a+1\\); distinct values of \\(a\\) give distinct diagonal constants, so we would need at least \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines.  \nIf instead a point \\((a,1)\\) is covered by a horizontal line, then that line is \\(y=1\\); but a single horizontal line can cover at most one of the points \\((a,1)\\) with different \\(a\\).  \nConsequently, to cover all \\(n-1\\) points we need at least \\(n-1\\) lines among the horizontal and diagonal families.  \nAn identical argument applied to the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n-1)\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) lines are needed among the vertical and diagonal families.  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nh+d\\ge n-1,\\qquad v+d\\ge n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two inequalities and using \\(h+v+d=n-1\\) from (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+d\\le 2(h+v+d)=2(n-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\(d\\ge n-1\\).  Substituting \\(d\\ge n-1\\) into (2) yields \\(h+v\\le 0\\), which forces \\(h=v=0\\).  This contradicts (3), because (3) requires \\(h+d\\ge n-1\\) with \\(d\\ge n-1\\) and \\(h=0\\) would give equality only if \\(d=n-1\\); then the second inequality \\(v+d\\ge n-1\\) would force \\(v\\ge 0\\) but we already have \\(v=0\\).  The only way to satisfy both (3) and (2) simultaneously is \\(h=v=d=n-1\\), which would give \\(h+v+d=3(n-1)>n-1\\), contradicting (2).  \n\nThus the assumption that a covering with exactly one sunny line exists leads to a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\nTherefore **\\(k=1\\) is impossible** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final classification  \n\nFrom (1) we have \\(k=0\\) attainable.  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) attainable.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k=1\\) cannot occur.  \nObviously \\(k>n\\) is impossible because only \\(n\\) lines are available.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
