e slope is \\(\\frac{0}{\\Delta x}=0\\) only for the original horizontal line, but by picking two points with different \\(x\\)-coordinates we obtain a non\u2011horizontal line; the slope is never \\(-1\\) because the points satisfy \\(a+i\\le n+1\\)).  \n\nThus after \\(k\\) such replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the required lattice points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nSince the configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  This gives the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\).\n\nThe construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, establishing the lower bound \\(0\\le k\\).  Consequently the set of admissible \\(k\\) is a (non\u2011empty) interval of integers containing both endpoints.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nWe must exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny*, i.e. their direction vectors are not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. A basic non\u2011sunny covering\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i^{(0)}&:\\; y=i\\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\nL_n^{(0)}&:\\; x=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (1) are non\u2011sunny: the first \\(n-1\\) are horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and the last one is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n*Claim.*  The family \\(\\{L_i^{(0)}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(L_b^{(0)}\\).  If \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), i.e. on \\(L_n^{(0)}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family. \u220e\n\nThus the configuration (1) satisfies the covering requirement with **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \nThe set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the horizontal line \\(L_i^{(0)}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(a,i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nPick any two distinct points of \\(H_i\\); for definiteness choose  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,i),\\qquad P_2=(n+1-i,i).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i^{(1)}\\) determined by \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-i = \\frac{0}{\\,n-i\\,}(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=i .\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) is precisely the original horizontal line, which is not sunny.  \nTo obtain a sunny line we must choose a *different* pair of points of \\(H_i\\) whose connecting line is not horizontal, not vertical, and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nBecause \\(|H_i|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(i\\le n-1\\), we can select two points  \n\n\\[\nQ_1=(a_1,i),\\qquad Q_2=(a_2,i)\n\\]\n\nwith \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) and \\(a_1\\neq a_2-1\\).  \nThe line through \\(Q_1\\) and \\(Q_2\\) is still horizontal, so we must modify the construction: we replace one of the points by a point of a *different* row while preserving the covering of all points of \\(H_i\\).\n\nObserve that each point \\((a,i)\\in H_i\\) also belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  Hence we may keep the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in the family and discard the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) entirely, provided that for every \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present.\n\nWe therefore proceed as follows.  Starting from the basic family (1) we **remove** the horizontal line \\(L_i^{(0)}\\) and **add** the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x = n+1-i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V_i\\) is non\u2011sunny (vertical), but we will later replace a suitable subset of the vertical lines by sunny lines.  Crucially, the points of \\(H_i\\) are now covered because each \\((a,i)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-i\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is already present among the lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) of the original family).  Thus the covering property is preserved after the replacement (1) \\(\\to\\) (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Introducing sunny lines\n\nNow we have at our disposal the following collection of lines:\n\n* the vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\);\n* the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for those indices \\(j\\) that have **not** been removed in the previous step.\n\nThe total number of lines is still \\(n\\).  To obtain a sunny line we replace a vertical line \\(x=c\\) by a line of a different admissible slope that still passes through all points of the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nPick two points of (4) with distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates, say  \n\n\\[\nR_1=(c,b_1),\\qquad R_2=(c,b_2),\\qquad b_1<b_2 .\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(R_1\\) and \\(R_2\\) is vertical, so we must alter one of the points.  Choose instead  \n\n\\[\nR_1'=(c,b_1),\\qquad R_2'=(c+1,b_2).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((c+1,b_2)\\in T_n\\) (the inequality \\(c+1+b_2\\le n+1\\) holds by the definition of the set (4)), the line \\(S_{c}\\) determined by \\(R_1'\\) and \\(R_2'\\) is a legitimate line in the plane.  Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{(c+1)-c}=b_2-b_1\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(m_c\\neq 0\\) (not horizontal), \\(m_c\\neq \\infty\\) (not vertical), and \\(m_c\\neq -1\\).  Consequently \\(S_c\\) is a sunny line.\n\nMoreover, every point of the set (4) lies on either the original vertical line \\(x=c\\) or on the new sunny line \\(S_c\\):  \n\n* the point \\((c,b_1)\\) is on \\(S_c\\) by construction;  \n* any other point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq b_1\\) lies on the original vertical line \\(x=c\\), which we have **removed**; however, because the line \\(S_c\\) contains the two distinct points \\((c,b_1)\\) and \\((c+1,b_2)\\), it also contains the point \\((c,b)\\) for every integer \\(b\\) between \\(b_1\\) and \\(b_2\\) (the three points are collinear).  By choosing \\(b_1\\) and \\(b_2\\) so that the interval \\([b_1,b_2]\\) covers all admissible \\(b\\)-values (i.e. \\(b_1=1\\) and \\(b_2=n+1-c\\)), the line \\(S_c\\) passes through **all** points of (4).\n\nThus we may replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) without losing any coverage of points in \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nStarting from the basic non\u2011sunny covering (1) we perform the following algorithm:\n\n1. For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) decide whether the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) will be kept.  \n   *If it is removed*, we keep the vertical line \\(x=n+1-i\\) (as in \u00a72).  \n\n2. After step\u202f1 we have a family consisting of all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) together with those horizontal lines that were not removed.  \n\n3. Choose any set \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of cardinality \\(k\\).  \n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) constructed in \u00a73.\n\nThe resulting family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_c\\) for \\(c\\in C\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with the retained horizontal lines).  By the arguments of \u00a7\u00a72 and\u202f3 every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.  All lines are distinct because each replacement changes the slope, and the slopes of the sunny lines \\(S_c\\) are all different (they equal \\(b_2-b_1\\) with distinct choices of the pair \\((b_1,b_2)\\)).\n\nSince the set \\(C\\) can be any subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(k\\), the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThe configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n\\) for any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a (non\u2011empty) interval of integers containing both endpoints \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  The precise description of this interval (whether every integer between the endpoints actually occurs) remains an open question."
