            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_{d}\\}_{d=2}^{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A uniform bound for any non‑sunny line\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a non‑sunny line (its slope is \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\)). Then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_{d}\\;(2\\le d\\le n+1)\\) in at most one point, and therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll diagonals have slope \\(-1\\). If \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points of the same diagonal, those two points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the fact that \\(\\ell\\) is not of slope \\(-1\\). Hence at most one point of each diagonal lies on \\(\\ell\\). There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, giving (1). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points on each diagonal\n\nFor a fixed diagonal \\(D_{d}\\) the points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on it are  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}\\cap S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=d\\},\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\n|D_{d}\\cap S_{n}| = d-1\\qquad (2\\le d\\le n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of a sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N = \\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal N| = n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsider an arbitrary diagonal \\(D_{d}\\).  \nEach line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can contribute at most one point of \\(D_{d}\\) (Lemma 1), so the total number of points of \\(D_{d}\\) that can be covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most \\(|\\mathcal N| = n-k\\).  \nSince every point of \\(D_{d}\\cap S_{n}\\) must be covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nd-1 = |D_{d}\\cap S_{n}| \\;\\le\\; n-k \\qquad\\text{for all } d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe most restrictive instance of (3) is obtained for the largest diagonal, \\(d=n+1\\), giving\n\n\\[\nn \\le n-k \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; k = 0 .\n\\]\n\nThis contradicts the assumption \\(k\\ge 1\\). Therefore no covering family can contain a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nThe construction of §1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nThe argument of §§2–4 proves that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_{n}\\) must have \\(k=0\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
