al lines together with the remaining sunny line on that diagonal cover **all** lattice points of the diagonal, exactly as explained in steps\u202f4\u20135.  \n\n   Hence the family (6.1) satisfies the covering requirement, and it contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)** \u2013  \n   There are only \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, so we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) is attainable.\n\n8. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction (6.1) provides \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n   Conversely, \\(k>n\\) is impossible.  \n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nD_{s}:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\nthe diagonal that contains the points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); the longest diagonal (\\(s=n+1\\)) therefore has \\(n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor every integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nintersects exactly one diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) in a lattice point, namely the diagonal  \n\n\\[\ns_{i}=1\\pmod{i+1},\\qquad 2\\le s_{i}\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSolving the system \\(\\{y=i x+1-i,\\;x+y=s\\}\\) yields (2.2) of the sketch:\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s+i-1}{i+1},\\qquad y=\\frac{i(s-1)}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe intersection is integral iff \\(i+1\\mid s+i-1\\), i.e. iff \\(s\\equiv1\\pmod{i+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one such \\(s\\); denote it by \\(s_{i}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,s-t)\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lying on \\(D_{s}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the definition of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and of \\(D_{s}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Sunny lines.** Keep the first \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each of them meets a distinct diagonal in a lattice point; their slopes are \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\), none of which equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence these \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines.** Replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{k+1},V_{k+2},\\dots ,V_{n},\\qquad\n   V_{t}:\\;x=t .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   A vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is covered\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nLet \\(i\\) be the unique index with \\(s=s_{i}\\) given by Lemma\u202f1.  \n\n*If \\(i\\le k\\)*, then the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) and, by Lemma\u202f1, contains the lattice point \\((a_{i},s-a_{i})\\) where  \n\n\\[\na_{i}= \\frac{s+i-1}{i+1}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,s-1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(i>k\\)*, then the line \\(L_{i}\\) has been replaced by the vertical line \\(V_{i}\\).  \nSince \\(i\\in