e sunny, because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total.  \n   Thus \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n6. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line whose abscissa equals its first coordinate, so the covering condition holds.  \n   All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny; this yields \\(k=0\\).\n\n7. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with a single sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose any slope \\(m\\) with \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance  \n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = 2(x-n)+1 = 2x-2n+1 .\n   \\]\n   \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n   The vertical lines cover every column \\(C_a\\) for \\(a\\le n-1\\) completely, and \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) covers the lone point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Hence all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Suppose a covering family contained two distinct sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can intersect at most one point of any column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \n   Since the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) each contain at least two points, Corollary\u202f2 forces the presence of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   That already accounts for \\(n-1\\) lines, leaving room for only **one** additional line; a second sunny line would exceed the allowed total of \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Hence a covering configuration cannot have two or more sunny lines.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   The covering condition forces the presence of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The remaining line may be either the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (giving \\(k=0