stinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nv:=|\\{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\mid \\ell\\text{ is vertical}\\,\\}|,\\qquad\ns:=n-v\n\\]\n\nso that \\(s\\) is the number of non‑vertical lines.\n\nFix a column \\(C_a\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(s\\) non‑vertical lines can contribute **at most one** point of \\(C_a\\); therefore at most \\(s\\) points of \\(C_a\\) can be covered by non‑vertical lines.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(|C_a|-s\\) points (if any) must be covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); in other words\n\n\\[\n\\text{If }|C_a|>s\\text{ then the line }x=a\\text{ belongs to }\\mathcal F .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow consider column \\(a=1\\).  From (2) we have \\(|C_1|=n\\).  \nSince there are only \\(s\\le n-1\\) non‑vertical lines (otherwise we would have no vertical line at all, which cannot cover the whole set), we have \\(|C_1|>s\\).  \nBy (4) the vertical line \\(x=1\\) must be present; thus \\(v\\ge 1\\).\n\nRemove column 1 and the line \\(x=1\\) from the problem.  \nWhat remains is the set \\(\\{C_a\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\}\\) together with the same collection of non‑vertical lines (still \\(s\\) of them).  \nApplying the same reasoning to the new first column (which is \\(C_2\\) and has size \\(n-1\\)) we obtain that \\(x=2\\) must also be present, because \\(n-1> s\\) (the inequality \\(s\\le n-2\\) follows from the previous step).  \n\nRepeating this argument inductively we see that for every \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is forced to belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\nv\\ge n-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad s=n-v\\le 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Bounding the number of sunny lines\n\nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis is not sunny, because “sunny’’ means “not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)”.  \nAll vertical lines are therefore non‑sunny.  \n\nFrom (5) at most one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) can be non‑vertical; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Constructions showing that the bounds are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n  No line is sunny, giving a covering with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Choose any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = 2x-2n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Its slope \\(2\\) avoids the three forbidden directions, so \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is sunny.  \n  Together the \\(n\\) lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly one line is sunny, establishing the case \\(k=1\\).\n\nBoth constructions satisfy the required covering condition, so the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at least two sunny lines.  \nSunny lines are non‑vertical, hence the family would contain at least two non‑vertical lines, i.e. \\(s\\ge 2\\).  \nThis contradicts the bound \\(s\\le 1\\) obtained in (5).  \nTherefore no covering with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nThe analysis above shows that the covering requirement forces at most one line to be non‑vertical, and consequently at most one line can be sunny.  Both possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) occur, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]"
