h lines, so far we have used \\(n+1\\) lines; we must delete two of them.\n\n   *Step 3.* Delete the two lines \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-2}\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still cover every point of \\(T_n\\) because any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on \\(\\ell_1\\) (if \\(b=0\\)), on \\(\\ell_2\\) (if \\(a=0\\)), or on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\).  \n\n   *Step 4.* Replace any \\(k\\) of the remaining non‑sunny lines (except the two axis lines, which we keep non‑sunny) by distinct sunny lines that still pass through the same set of points.  \n   For a fixed sum \\(s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n\\)) the points on the line \\(x+y=s\\) are\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Choose a sunny line through any two of these points (possible because the slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, but any other slope can be realised).  \n   Because a sunny line can contain at most two points of the same diagonal, we replace each diagonal line by a sunny line that passes through exactly the same two points; the remaining points of that diagonal are already covered by other lines (the axis lines or previously replaced diagonals).  \n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) diagonals yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. Since we started with \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines (the two axes plus the \\(n-4\\) untouched diagonals), the number of sunny lines can be any integer\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)** –  \n   Suppose a configuration had at most one non‑sunny line.  \n   Count the incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Each sunny line can contain at most two of them (otherwise its slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n   With at most one non‑sunny line, the total number of incidences would be at most \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\).  \n   However, each of the three points must lie on at least one line, and the set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points, giving a lower bound of \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) incidences, which for \\(n\\ge3\\) exceeds \\(2n-1\\).  \n   Hence at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, and consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nThey belong to \\(T_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and are not collinear.\n\n* If a line is sunny, its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can contain at most two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \n* A non‑sunny line can contain at most one of them, because the three forbidden slopes are precisely the slopes of the three lines determined by the pairs \\(\\{P_1,P_2\\},\\{P_1,P_3\\},\\{P_2,P_3\\}\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one is non‑sunny.  \nThen the total number of incidences between the lines and the set \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)+1 = 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut each of the three points must be covered, so we need at least three incidences.  \nMore importantly, the whole set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, each of which must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines; hence the total number of incidences is at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the assumption is impossible; at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with exactly two non‑sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and then show how to reduce the number of sunny lines down to \\(0\\).\n\n#### 2.1  A base configuration with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_1 : y=0\\)   (horizontal, non‑sunny);\n* \\(\\ell_2 : x=0\\)   (vertical, non‑sunny);\n* for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) let  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_{s}: x+y=s .\n  \\]\n  These have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.\n\nAltogether we have \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) lines; we delete the two lines \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-2}\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n\\) lines are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n-3\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_n\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Claim.** \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_2\\) or \\(\\ell_1\\) respectively.*  \nOtherwise \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=s\\) for some integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\le n-3\\) the point lies on \\(\\ell_s\\) which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); if \\(s=n\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_n\\), also present.  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the two axis lines are non‑sunny, while each diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\) (with slope \\(-1\\)) is also non‑sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines and **zero** sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Turning diagonal lines into sunny ones\n\nFix a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n-3\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose any two distinct points of \\(D_s\\), say \\((i,s-i)\\) and \\((j,s-j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-i)-(s-j)}{i-j}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden. To obtain a sunny line we must pick a *different* pair of points that do **not** lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nObserve that for each \\(s\\) the set \\(D_s\\) contains at least three points (since \\(s\\ge 3\\) for the diagonals we will replace).  \nPick the first two points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((2,s-2)\\); the line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-1)-(s-2)}{1-2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso we cannot use this pair. Instead take \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((3,s-3)\\) (possible because \\(s\\ge4\\) when we replace). Their slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-1)-(s-3)}{1-3}= \\frac{2}{-2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nstill forbidden. The trick is to **replace the diagonal by a sunny line that passes through exactly two points of the diagonal and through no third point of the same diagonal**.  \n\nBecause the forbidden slopes are only three numbers, we can always find a line through any two points of \\(D_s\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) simply by *perturbing* one of the points infinitesimally. However we must keep the line passing through *grid* points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA constructive way is the following: for each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) we keep the two extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\). The line through them has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + s .\n\\]\n\nTo obtain a sunny line we replace it by the line  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + s + \\varepsilon ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a non‑zero real number chosen so small that the line still passes through the two extreme integer points (this is impossible because the line is determined by its two points). Hence we need a different approach.\n\n**Correct approach.**  \nInstead of trying to keep the same two points, we keep **one** point of the diagonal and replace the diagonal by a sunny line that passes through that point and through a point belonging to another diagonal already covered.  \n\nSpecifically, for each \\(s\\) we keep the point \\((1,s-1)\\).  \nConsider the sunn