------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with exactly two non‑sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and then show how to reduce the number of sunny lines down to \\(0\\).\n\n#### 2.1  A base configuration with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_1 : y=0\\)   (horizontal, non‑sunny);\n* \\(\\ell_2 : x=0\\)   (vertical, non‑sunny);\n* for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) let  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_{s}: x+y=s .\n  \\]\n  These have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.\n\nAltogether we have \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) lines; we delete the two lines \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-2}\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n\\) lines are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n-3\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_n\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Claim.** \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_2\\) or \\(\\ell_1\\) respectively.*  \nOtherwise \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=s\\) for some integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\le n-3\\) the point lies on \\(\\ell_s\\) which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); if \\(s=n\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_n\\), also present.  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the two axis lines are non‑sunny, while each diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\) (with slope \\(-1\\)) is also non‑sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines and **zero** sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Turning diagonal lines into sunny ones\n\nFix a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n-3\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose any two distinct points of \\(D_s\\), say \\((i,s-i)\\) and \\((j,s-j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-i)-(s-j)}{i-j}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden. To obtain a sunny line we must pick a *different* pair of points that do **not** lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nObserve that for each \\(s\\) the set \\(D_s\\) contains at least three points (since \\(s\\ge 3\\) for the diagonals we will replace).  \nPick the first two points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((2,s-2)\\); the line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-1)-(s-2)}{1-2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso we cannot use this pair. Instead take \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((3,s-3)\\) (possible because \\(s\\ge4\\) when we replace). Their slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(s-1)-(s-3)}{1-3}= \\frac{2}{-2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nstill forbidden. The trick is to **replace the diagonal by a sunny line that passes through exactly two points of the diagonal and through no third point of the same diagonal**.  \n\nBecause the forbidden slopes are only three numbers, we can always find a line through any two points of \\(D_s\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) simply by *perturbing* one of the points infinitesimally. However we must keep the line passing through *grid* points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA constructive way is the following: for each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) we keep the two extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\). The line through them has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + s .\n\\]\n\nTo obtain a sunny line we replace it by the line  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + s + \\varepsilon ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a non‑zero real number chosen so small that the line still passes through the two extreme integer points (this is impossible because the line is determined by its two points). Hence we need a different approach.\n\n**Correct approach.**  \nInstead of trying to keep the same two points, we keep **one** point of the diagonal and replace the diagonal by a sunny line that passes through that point and through a point belonging to another diagonal already covered.  \n\nSpecifically, for each \\(s\\) we keep the point \\((1,s-1)\\).  \nConsider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\gamma_s : y = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + 1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(2\\le s\\le n-3\\).  \nThe line \\(\\gamma_s\\) passes through \\((1,s-1)\\) (check: \\(s-1 = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\cdot 1 +1\\)).  \nIt also passes through \\((s-1,1)\\) because  \n\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,(s-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\gamma_s\\) contains the two extreme points of the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) but **does not contain any interior point** of that diagonal, because any interior point \\((i,s-i)\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le s-2\\) would satisfy  \n\n\\[\ns-i = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,i +1 \\Longleftrightarrow (s-1)(s-i) = (s-2)i + (s-1) ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(s^2 - s i - s + i = s i -2i + s -1\\), i.e. \\(s^2 -2s +1 = 2i(s-1)\\).  \nSince the left‑hand side is \\((s-1)^2\\) and the right‑hand side is a multiple of \\(2(s-1)\\), equality would require \\((s-1)=2i\\), impossible because \\(s-1\\) is odd when \\(s\\) is even and vice‑versa; in any case the equality does not hold for integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le s-2\\). Hence no interior point lies on \\(\\gamma_s\\).\n\nConsequently, after replacing the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) by the sunny line \\(\\gamma_s\\) we still cover all points of \\(D_s\\) (the two extremes are covered by \\(\\gamma_s\\); the interior points are already covered by other diagonals or by the axis lines).  \n\nPerform this replacement for any chosen subset of the diagonals.  \nIf we replace exactly \\(k\\) diagonals, we obtain a configuration consisting of  \n\n* the two axis lines (non‑sunny);\n* the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\gamma_s\\).\n\nThus the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\), and the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).  \n\nSince we may choose any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have constructed, for each such \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Section 1 we already derived inequality (1): any admissible configuration must contain at least two non‑sunny lines. Hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n\nTherefore no configuration exists for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]"
