s to have **every** line be sunny; otherwise a\ncolumn missed by a sunny line would have to be covered by a vertical line, which\nwould be non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that both types appear.\nThus the presence of a sunny line forces \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence any feasible configuration must satisfy simultaneously \\(k=n\\) and\n\\(k\\le n/2\\), which is impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Therefore **no sunny line**\ncan appear, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are\ndistinct.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument shows that a configuration containing any sunny line is\nimpossible, while the explicit construction above shows that a configuration\nwith no sunny line exists.  Consequently the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
