mber of sunny lines.*  \n   Fix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   – Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); they cover **all**\n   points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n   – For each remaining column \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a distinct\n   sunny line that passes through **all** points of that column except the\n   lowest one.  Such a line exists because the points of a fixed column have\n   distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates; pick any two of them whose slope is neither\n   \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (possible because the column contains at least\n   two points when \\(a\\le n-1\\)).  The line therefore contains every point of\n   the column except the one with smallest \\(y\\).  \n   – The omitted lowest points are \\((a,1)\\) for \\(a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\); they are\n   all distinct and lie on the non‑sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope\n   \\(-1\\)).  Add this diagonal as the **last** line of the family.\n\n   The resulting family consists of  \n\n   * \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines,\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines (one for each of the columns \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\)),\n   * one diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   Since \\(n-k + k + 1 = n+1\\) lines have been produced, we delete the\n   diagonal line when \\(k=n-1\\) (in that case the sunny lines already cover the\n   whole set).  In every case we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which\n   precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of\n   them.  Hence each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that a configuration cannot contain \\(n\\) sunny lines; the\n   counting argument together with Lemma 1 yields the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n   The explicit constructions above realise every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\)\n   and \\(n-1\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
