ny lines \\(S_c\\) (\\(c=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\));\n   * one diagonal line \\(D_{-1}\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   Hence the total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k+1=n+1\\).  When \\(k=n-1\\) the\n   set \\(D\\) contains a single point, which is already covered by the sunny\n   line for that column; in this case we simply omit the diagonal line and are\n   left with exactly \\(n\\) lines.  For every \\(k\\le n-2\\) we delete any one of\n   the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (which does not affect the covering because the\n   points of its column are already covered by the sunny lines) and obtain a\n   family of precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a set\n   of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that a configuration cannot contain \\(n\\) sunny lines; the\n   counting argument together with Lemma 1 yields the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n   \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]"
