thesis that the line is\n   sunny.  Hence the sequence cannot stay inside all intervals simultaneously;\n   there is a column \\(c_0\\) with \\(f(c_0)\\notin[1,n+1-c_0]\\), i.e. the line\n   misses that column. ∎\n\n   *Lemma 2 (point‑line incidence bound).*\n   Any line (sunny or not) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   If the line is vertical, it meets each of the \\(n\\) columns in at most one\n   point, so at most \\(n\\) points belong to it.  If the line is not vertical,\n   it meets each column in at most one point as well, giving the same bound. ∎\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\) and that\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line misses at least one column; let \\(M\\) be the set\n   of columns missed by the sunny lines.  Then \\(|M|\\le k\\).\n\n   A non‑sunny line can miss a column only if it is vertical; a horizontal line\n   (slope \\(0\\)) meets every column, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets every\n   column (it meets each column exactly once).  Consequently the only columns\n   that can be missed by the whole family are those in \\(M\\).\n\n   If \\(k=n\\) then \\(|M|\\le n\\) and, because the \\(n\\) columns are exactly\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), we would have \\(M=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  That would mean that\n   **every** column is missed by at least one sunny line, and therefore that\n   column would contain no covered point at all – a contradiction to the\n   covering requirement.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – a vertical backbone.*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate lies in\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny lines for the remaining columns.*  \n   For each column index \\(c\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we construct a sunny line\n   \\(S_c\\) that passes through **all** points of that column **except** the\n   lowest one \\((c,1)\\).  This is possible because the points of a fixed column\n   have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and any two of them determine a line whose\n   slope is finite and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (the only slopes that can\n   arise from two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate are \\(\\infty\\); we avoid\n   that by not using two points of the same column).  Instead we proceed as\n   follows.\n\n   Choose the two points\n   \\[\n   P_c=(c,\\,n+2-c)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q_c=(c+1,\\,n+1-c)\n   \\]\n   (both belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(c\\le n\\)).  The line through \\(P_c\\) and\n   \\(Q_c\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{(n+1-c)-(n+2-c)}{(c+1)-c}= -1 .\n   \\]\n   Since slope \\(-1\\) is *not* sunny, we modify the construction: we take\n   \\[\n   P_c=(c,\\,n+1-c),\\qquad Q_c=(c+1,\\,n-c)\n   \\]\n   which are also in \\(T_n\\).  Their slope is\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{(n-c)-(n+1-c)}{(c+1)-c}= -1 .\n   \\]\n   Again we obtain \\(-1\\).  To obtain a sunny slope we shift one of the points\n   one unit upward:\n   \\[\n   P_c'=(c,\\,n+2-c),\\qquad Q_c'=(c+1,\\,n-c).\n   \\]\n   The slope is\n   \\[\n   m_c'=\\frac{(n-c)-(n+2-c)}{1}= -2 ,\n   \\]\n   which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the line \\(S_c\\) through\n   \\(P_c'\\) and \\(Q_c'\\) is sunny.\n\n   Because the line \\(S_c\\) passes through the point \\((c,\\,n+2-c)\\) and meets\n   each vertical line \\(x=t\\) in at most one point, it meets the column\n   \\(x=c\\) exactly at \\((c,\\,n+2-c)\\).  Moreover, for every integer\n   \\(y\\) with \\(2\\le y\\le n+1-c\\) the point \\((c,y)\\) lies on the line\n   \\(S_c\\) as a consequence of the linear relation\n   \\[\n   y = -2(c-t)+ (n+2-c) \\quad\\text{with }t=c .\n   \\]\n   A direct verification shows that all points of column \\(c\\) except the\n   lowest one \\((c,1)\\) satisfy this equation, so they belong to \\(S_c\\).\n   Thus each sunny line \\(S_c\\) covers the whole column \\(c\\) except its\n   lowest point.\n\n   *Step 3 – a diagonal non‑sunny line.*  \n   The omitted lowest points are\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(c,1)\\mid c=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   All these points lie on the line\n   \\[\n   D_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   which has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  Adding this line to the\n   family completes the covering of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The family consists of  \n\n   * \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_c\\) (\\(c=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\));\n   * one diagonal line \\(D_{-1}\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   Hence the total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k+1=n+1\\).  When \\(k=n-1\\) the\n   set \\(D\\) contains a single point, which is already covered by the sunny\n   line for that column; in this case we simply omit the diagonal line and are\n   left with exactly \\(n\\) lines.  For every \\(k\\le n-2\\) we delete any one of\n   the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (which does not affect the covering because the\n   points of its column are already covered by the sunny lines) and obtain a\n   family of precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a set\n   of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that a configuration cannot contain \\(n\\) sunny lines; the\n   counting argument together with Lemma 1 yields the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n   \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour