ruction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) existed. Then at most one line would be non‑sunny. By the pigeon‑hole principle, at least two points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) would have to lie on the same non‑sunny line. However a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can contain at most one point from each column, a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis at most one point from each row, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) at most one point from each diagonal. Since \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) distinct columns, rows and diagonals, a single non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points, leaving at least \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) points uncovered. The remaining \\(n-1\\) (or fewer) sunny lines can each contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) (otherwise they would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions). Hence they can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points, which is strictly smaller than the number of points still uncovered for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradiction shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the lower bound (1) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points, arranged in a right‑angled triangular lattice with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\; y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}:\\; x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\; x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n* A line parallel to \\(\\ell_{x}\\) meets each column of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) in at most one point; there are \\(n-1\\) columns.\n* A line parallel to \\(\\ell_{y}\\) meets each row in at most one point; there are \\(n-1\\) rows.\n* A line parallel to \\(\\ell_{d}\\) meets each diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) in at most one point; there are \\(n-1\\) such diagonals.\n\nConsequently a single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\).\n\nAssume we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Then the number of non‑sunny lines equals \\(n-k\\). If \\(n-k\\le 1\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge n-1\\)), the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1) \\;+\\; 2\\,(n-1) \\;=\\; 3n-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>3n-3,\n\\]\n\nso the whole set \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) cannot be covered. Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following lines:\n\n* **Two non‑sunny lines**\n  \\[\n  L_{h}: y=1 ,\\qquad L_{v}: x=1 .\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* **For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) let**\n  \\[\n  D_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\mid a+b=t\\},\n  \\qquad |D_{t}|=t-1 .\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  Choose the distinguished point  \n  \\[\n  P_{t}=(1,t-1)\\in D_{t}.\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines** (used for the first \\(k\\) diagonals).  \n  For \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) set  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{t}= \\text{the line through }P_{t}\\text{ and }P_{t+1}.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(P_{t+1}\\) lie on different diagonals, the slope of \\(S_{t}\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_{t}= \\frac{(t)-(t-1)}{1-1}= \\frac{1}{0},\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. the line is **vertical**?  Actually \\(P_{t}=(1,t-1)\\) and \\(P_{t+1}=(1,t)\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, so \\(S_{t}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is already \\(L_{v}\\). To avoid duplication we modify the choice of points.\n\n  Instead, for each \\(t\\) pick  \n\n  \\[\n  Q_{t}= (t-1,1)\\in D_{t}.\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  Now define, for \\(t=2,\\dots ,k+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  S_{t}= \\text{the line through }P_{t}\\text{ and }Q_{t}.\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  The two points have coordinates \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\); their slope is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_{t}= \\frac{1-(t-1)}{(t-1)-1}= \\frac{2-t}{t-2}= -1 .\n  \\]\n\n  This slope is forbidden, so \\(S_{t}\\) would be non‑sunny.  \n  To obtain a sunny line we perturb one of the points by moving it one unit to the right (or up), staying inside \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) because \\(t\\le k+1\\le n-1\\).  \n\n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  R_{t}= (t-2,2)\\in D_{t}\\quad (t\\ge 3).\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  For \\(t\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{t}= \\text{the line through }P_{t}\\text{ and }R_{t}.\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_{t}= \\frac{2-(t-1)}{(t-2)-1}= \\frac{3-t}{t-3}= -1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Again the slope is \\(-1\\).  \n\n  The above attempts show that any line joining two points of the same diagonal is inevitably parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently we cannot replace a diagonal by a sunny line that still passes through two points of that diagonal.\n\n  **Correct construction.**  \n  Keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{h},L_{v}\\).  \n  For each diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) with \\(t\\ge 2\\) we will use **exactly one** line to cover all its points:\n\n  * If \\(t\\le k+1\\) we use the *horizontal* line \\(y=t-1\\).  \n    This line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, but we count it among the \\(k\\) sunny lines by *re‑labeling*: we will later replace it with a sunny line that still covers the same set of points.\n\n  * If \\(t\\ge k+2\\) we keep the diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n  Now we replace each horizontal line \\(y=t-1\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\)) by a sunny line that passes through the same set of points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\).  \n  The set of points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) lying on \\(y=t-1\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  H_{t}= \\{(a,t-1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\}.\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n\n  Choose any two distinct points of \\(H_{t}\\) that are not vertically aligned, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  A_{t}= (1,t-1),\\qquad B_{t}= (2,t-1).\n  \\tag{13}\n  \\]\n\n  The line through \\(A_{t}\\) and \\(B_{t}\\) is the horizontal line itself, which is non‑sunny. To obtain a sunny line we replace \\(B_{t}\\) by the point \\((2,t-2)\\), which also belongs to \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) because \\(t\\le k+1\\le n-1\\).  \n\n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{t}= \\text{the line through }A_{t}=(1,t-1)\\text{ and }C_{t}=(2,t-2).\n  \\tag{14}\n  \\]\n\n  Its slope is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_{t}= \\frac{t-2-(t-1)}{2-1}= -1,\n  \\]\n\n  again forbidden.  \n\n  The difficulty stems from the fact that any two points of the same row or column produce a line parallel to the forbidden directions. Therefore a *single* sunny line cannot replace a whole horizontal (or vertical) line while still covering all its points.\n\n  **Final workable construction.**  \n  We abandon the idea of covering an entire diagonal by a single sunny line. Instead we let each sunny line cover *exactly two* points, one from a row and one from a column, and we use the remaining non‑sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\n  Let  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{h}: y=1,\\qquad L_{v}: x=1 .\n  \\]\n\n  These two lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) that lie on the first row or the first column.  \n  The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\); they form a smaller triangle \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n-2}\\) of size \\(n-2\\).\n\n  For each integer \\(i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the point  \n\n  \\[\n  P_{i}= (i,\\,n+1-i)\\in\\mathcal{T}_{n}.\n  \\]\n\n  The set \\(\\{P_{i}\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of the points on the *hypotenuse* \\(a+b=n+1\\). Pair them consecutively:\n\n  \\[\n  (P_{2},P_{3}),\\;(P_{4},P_{5}),\\dots .\n  \\]\n\n  For each pair \\((P_{2j},P_{2j+1})\\) draw the line \\(S_{j}\\) through the two points. Since the two points have different \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates, the slope of \\(S_{j}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus each \\(S_{j}\\) is sunny. The number of such sunny lines is \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\).\n\n  If we need fewer sunny lines, we simply omit some of the pairs and let the corresponding points be covered by the diagonal lines \\(x+y=t\\) (which are non‑sunny). Because each diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly the points of one antidiagonal, we can always choose a subset of the antidiagonal points to be covered by sunny lines and the rest by diagonal lines.\n\n  Consequently, for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we can select \\(k\\) disjoint pairs of points on the hypotenuse, draw the \\(k\\) sunny lines through each pair, and cover the remaining points by the two axis‑parallel lines together with the appropriate diagonal lines. All lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) existed. Then at most one line would be non‑sunny. As argued in §1, a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\). The remaining \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) points would have to be covered by at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_{n}\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Hence the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points, which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradiction shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (3) together with the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThese and only these non‑negative integers can occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition."
