                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, but none of these lines can be sunny; at least two of the lines must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Geometry of the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of the lattice points of a right‑angled triangular array with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n   It contains \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points.\n\n2. **A counting lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.*  \n   A line that is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   If a sunny line contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) then the three points would be collinear.  \n   The differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) for the three pairs would all be proportional.  \n   Because all three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) and have integer coordinates, the only possible common direction vectors are \\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎  \n\n3. **A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Any line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis meets each column of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) in at most one point; there are \\(n-1\\) columns.  \n   Similarly a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis meets each row in at most one point, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) meets each diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)) in at most one point.  \n   Consequently **any single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points** of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n   Suppose we had a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Then the number of non‑sunny lines equals \\(n-k\\).  \n   If \\(k\\ge 1\\) then at most \\(n-1\\) points can be covered by the non‑sunny lines, and the remaining \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By the Lemma each sunny line covers at most two points, so the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \n   Hence we would need  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right–hand side of (1) is at least \\(3\\), while the left–hand side is at most \\(2(n-1)\\).  \n   Direct inspection shows that (1) cannot hold for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Therefore **no sunny line can appear**; i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n4. **Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the two axis‑parallel lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}: y=1,\\qquad L_{v}: x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   and for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}: x+y=t .\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{h},L_{v}\\}\\cup\\{L_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a=1\\) (hence lies on \\(L_{h}\\)), or \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on \\(L_{v}\\)), or \\(a+b=t\\) for some \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on \\(L_{t}\\)).  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all required points, and all its lines are non‑sunny.  \n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\nCombining the impossibility of any sunny line with the explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\nA line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\(P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nBecause the three points are collinear, the vectors \\(P_1P_2\\) and \\(P_1P_3\\) are linearly dependent; hence there exists a non‑zero integer pair \\((u,v)\\) such that each difference \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) is a multiple of \\((u,v)\\).  \nSince all three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) and have integer coordinates, the only possible primitive direction vectors are \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); any other primitive vector would force at least one of the points to lie outside the triangle.  \nThus the line would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the assumption that it is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct columns \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\) intersecting \\(\\mathcal T_n\\); a horizontal line meets each column in at most one point, so it contains at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nThe same argument applies to vertical lines (rows) and to lines of the form \\(x+y=t\\) (diagonals), of which there are also \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.  \nLet \\(s=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Maximum number of points covered by non‑sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, hence all non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(s\\,(n-1)\\) points.\n\n*Maximum number of points covered by sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points, so the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_n|\n\\;=\\;\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\n\\;\\le\\; s\\,(n-1)+2k\n\\;=\\;(n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (3) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\le (n-1)n - (n-3)k,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\nk\\le \\frac{(n-1)n- (n-1)n/2}{\\,n-3\\,}\n= \\frac{(n-1)n}{2(n-3)} .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right‑hand side is strictly less than \\(1\\); consequently the only integer satisfying the inequality is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no sunny line can appear** in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* \\(L_{h}: y=1\\) (horizontal),\n* \\(L_{v}: x=1\\) (vertical),\n* for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) the diagonal line \\(L_{t}: x+y=t\\).\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny (each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{v}\\);  \nif \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{h}\\);  \notherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=t\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{t}\\).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines in the family.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) lines and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition derived from counting arguments forces \\(k=0\\); the explicit construction above shows that this value is attainable. Consequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
