Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   * Choose the two diagonals  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{n+1}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad D_{n}\n     \\]\n\n     as two of the required lines; they are non\u2011sunny.  \n   * For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) pick a sunny line \\(L_i\\) of slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) passing through the point \\((i,1)\\).  \n     Because the slopes are all distinct, the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise non\u2011parallel and none of them is parallel to the axes or to any diagonal, hence they are sunny.  \n   * The remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines are taken to be the diagonals  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{c}\\qquad\\text{for }c=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\text{ and }c\\neq n .\n     \\]\n\n     (There are exactly \\(n-2-k\\) such values of \\(c\\).)  \n   *Verification.*  \n     - Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\); thus it is covered by the line \\(D_{a+b}\\) unless \\(a+b\\) equals \\(n\\) or \\(n+1\\).  \n     - Points on \\(D_{n}\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) are covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines we have explicitly taken.  \n     - The sunny lines \\(L_i\\) are introduced only to increase the number of sunny lines to the prescribed \\(k\\); they do not destroy the covering property because they intersect the grid points already covered by the diagonals, and any point not on a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\le n-1\\) is already on \\(D_n\\) or \\(D_{n+1}\\). Hence the whole set \\(S\\) remains covered.  \n     - All \\(n\\) lines are distinct by construction.  \n\n   Thus we have produced, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1,n\\).**  \n   The argument of Lemma\u202f1 already shows that at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny; consequently a configuration with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit construction yields the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\nthe diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \nThe points of \\(S\\) lying on \\(D_c\\) are  \n\n\\[\nD_c\\cap S=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nhence \\(|D_c\\cap S|=c-1\\).  \nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n|D_{n+1}\\cap S|=n,\\qquad |D_{n}\\cap S|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then for every diagonal \\(D_c\\) we have \\(|L\\cap D_c|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) were parallel to \\(D_c\\) it would intersect it in either no point or infinitely many points; since \\(L\\) is sunny it is not parallel to \\(D_c\\). Two non\u2011parallel lines intersect in exactly one point, whence the claim. \u220e\n\nConsider the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\), which contains \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines of a configuration were sunny, each of them could meet \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1); consequently at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) could be covered, and equality would force **every** line to pass through a distinct point of \\(D_{n+1}\\). In that situation none of the remaining diagonals \\(D_c\\;(c\\le n)\\) could be fully covered, because a sunny line meets each of them in at most one point. Hence a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore at least one line must be parallel to a diagonal, i.e. non\u2011sunny.  \nApply the same reasoning to the second longest diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) (which has \\(n-1\\) points). If the only non\u2011sunny line were \\(D_{n+1}\\), then the points of \\(D_{n}\\) would have to be covered by sunny lines, each contributing at most one point; at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be needed, leaving at most one line for all other diagonals, which is impossible. Consequently a second non\u2011sunny line is necessary.\n\nThus any admissible configuration contains **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines, and consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n1. **Two non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}=D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\n   L_{2}=D_{n}  :\\;x+y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2+i}:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,(x-i)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_{2+i}\\) is sunny.  \n   The slopes are distinct, so the lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel; also none of them coincides with a diagonal because a diagonal has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n3. **Remaining non\u2011sunny diagonals.**  \n   For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) and \\(c\\neq n\\) (there are exactly \\(n-2-k\\) such values) include the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2+k+(c-2)} = D_c :\\; x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and distinct from the two diagonals already used.\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{the long diagonals})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{short diagonals}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n**Verification of the covering property.**  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Its sum \\(c=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(c=n+1\\) or \\(c=n\\) the point lies on \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\) respectively.  \n* If \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) and \\(c\\neq n\\) then the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\), which is one of the lines listed in step\u202f3.  \n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then exactly one line is non\u2011sunny, say it is parallel to a diagonal \\(D_c\\). All other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny and, by Lemma\u202f1, intersect each diagonal in at most one point. The diagonal \\(D_c\\) itself is covered (by the non\u2011sunny line), but the diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) with \\(c'\\neq c\\) contains at least two points of \\(S\\) (indeed \\(c'\\ge 3\\)), and therefore would require at least two distinct sunny lines to cover those two points. Repeating this argument for the \\(n-1\\) remaining diagonals shows that at least \\(n\\) sunny lines are needed, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are present. The same reasoning rules out the case \\(k=n\\). Hence no configuration exists for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable; and \u00a73 proves that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible. Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
