 \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) has any other slope, the set \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is a subset of a line of the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\).  \nBecause the projection of \\(T_n\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis has length \\(n\\), at most \\(n\\) distinct integer abscissae can appear, and the condition \\(m\\neq -1\\) forces the corresponding ordinates to be distinct as well; hence \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower‑bound Lemma  \n\n**Lemma 3.1**  \nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line among the \\(n\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n*Case 1 – No non‑sunny line.*  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny, i.e. their slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 each such line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the bound \\(n\\) is attainable only for slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\,(n-1)=n^{2}-n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 2 – Exactly one non‑sunny line.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be that line. By Lemma 2 it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the bound \\(n\\) is achieved only when \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus the total number of covered points does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)(n-1)=n^{2}-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-2n+1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nagain contradicting the requirement that all points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  \n\nBoth cases are impossible; therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, establishing (2). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction Lemma (Upper bound)  \n\n**Lemma 4.1**  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\). We construct the required family \\(\\mathcal L\\) as the union of three subfamilies.\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}: y=0 ,\\qquad L_{v}: x=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are horizontal and vertical, respectively, and are clearly non‑sunny.\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n\n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a distinct slope \\(m_i\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   (For instance \\(m_i=i\\).)  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=m_i x + c_i ,\n   \\qquad c_i = (n-i+1) - m_i (i+1) .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i+1,\\; n-i)\n   \\]\n\n   satisfies \\(P_i\\in S_i\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   n-i = m_i (i+1) + c_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(P_i\\in T_n\\) since  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+1)+(n-i)=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Distinctness of the slopes guarantees that the lines \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from \\(L_h,L_v\\).\n\n3. **\\(n-2-k\\) additional non‑sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)**  \n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2-k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t:\\; x+y = t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n   They are distinct because the constants \\(t+1\\) differ.\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Three mutually exclusive possibilities occur.\n\n* **(i) \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\).**  \n  The point belongs to \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_h\\), respectively.\n\n* **(ii) \\(a+b=n+1\\).**  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is one of the \\(D_t\\) (take \\(t=n-1\\); note that \\(n-1\\ge n-2-k\\) because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\n\n* **(iii) \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).**  \n  Write \\(s=a+b\\). Then \\(2\\le s\\le n\\).  \n\n  - If \\(s\\le n- k\\) then \\(s-1\\le n-1-k\\) and the line \\(D_{s-1}\\) (which belongs to the family of type‑3 lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n  - If \\(s> n- k\\) then \\(s=n+1-j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n    Consequently  \n\n    \\[\n    (a,b) = (i+1,\\,n-i) \\quad\\text{with } i=j-1 .\n    \\]\n\n    Hence \\((a,b)=P_{j}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_{j}\\).\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  \n\nCounting the lines, we have  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{horizontal, vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{slope }-1)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 3.1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 4.1 demonstrates that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the complete set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required arrangement of \\(n\\) lines is possible is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
