\n     S_i:\\; y=m_i x + c_i,\\qquad \n     c_i=(n-i+1)-m_i(i+1).\n     \\]\n\n     Then the point  \n\n     \\[\n     P_i=(i+1,\\;n-i)\n     \\]\n\n     lies on \\(S_i\\) and satisfies \\(P_i\\in T_n\\) because \\((i+1)+(n-i)=n+1\\).\n\n   * **\\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)**  \n\n     \\[\n     D_t:\\;x+y=t+1\\qquad (t=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n     \\]\n\n   * **One additional non‑sunny line**  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{n-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n   Counting gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(L_h,L_v)+k\\;(S_i)+(n-2-k)\\;(D_t)+1\\;(D_{n-1})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The only sunny lines are the \\(S_i\\), so exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\) the point lies on \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_h\\).  \n   * If \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \n   * Otherwise \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\).  \n     Write \\(s=a+b\\).  \n\n     – If \\(s\\le n- k\\) then \\(s-1\\le n-1-k\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{s-1}\\).  \n\n     – If \\(s> n- k\\) then \\(s=n+1-j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)=P_j\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma 4 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration attaining that value of \\(k\\).  \n   Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
