D_c|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Two non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nSuppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem, and let  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nt=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line can be parallel only to a diagonal (parallelism to the axes would leave the whole set \\(S\\) uncovered, because no point of \\(S\\) lies on a horizontal or vertical line).  \nHence every non‑sunny line is exactly one of the diagonals \\(D_c\\).  \nLet us denote by \\(\\mathcal D\\subset\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) the set of diagonals that appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(|\\mathcal D|=t\\).\n\nAll points of the diagonals belonging to \\(\\mathcal D\\) are already covered.  \nThe remaining points are those belonging to the complementary set of diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D^{c}=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal D .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\sum_{D_c\\in\\mathcal D^{c}}|D_c\\cap S|\n   =\\sum_{D_c\\in\\mathcal D^{c}}(c-1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can meet a given diagonal in at most one point, therefore a sunny line can contribute at most \\(|\\mathcal D^{c}|=n-t\\) points to the total of uncovered points.  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\,(n-t)\\) points, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-t)\\ge P .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k=n-t\\), inequality (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n(n-t)^{2}\\ge P .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sufficient condition\n\nTo make (6) as easy as possible we choose the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) to consist of the \\(t\\) longest diagonals, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir lengths are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\); their total contribution equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)=\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points in \\(S\\) is, by (3),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence, using (4)–(8),\n\n\\[\nP=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nInsert this expression for \\(P\\) into (6) and replace \\(t\\) by \\(n-k\\):\n\n\\[\nk^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-k)\\,(n+k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation gives\n\n\\[\nk^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k^{2}+k}{2}\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k(k-1)\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nThus (6) holds for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsequently the counting argument imposes **no further restriction** on \\(k\\); any such \\(k\\) is *a priori* admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the problem’s requirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery line is parallel to a diagonal, hence none is sunny, and by definition each point of \\(S\\) lies on the unique diagonal whose equation is \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (ii)  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=i\\,x+(i+1).\n\\]\n\nAll slopes \\(i\\) are distinct and none equals \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nSolving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=c,\\\\\ny=i\\,x+(i+1)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives the intersection point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{i},y_{i}\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{c-i-1}{i+1},\\; \