d t=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A non‑sunny line cannot be horizontal or vertical, because no point of \\(S\\) lies on a horizontal or vertical line; consequently every non‑sunny line must be one of the diagonals \\(D_{c}\\).  \n   Denote by \\(\\mathcal D\\subset\\{D_{2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) the set of diagonals that appear in the configuration; then \\(|\\mathcal D|=t\\).\n\n   All points of the diagonals in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are already covered.  \n   The still uncovered points are those belonging to the complementary set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D^{c}=\\{D_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal D .\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) the number of uncovered points equals  \n\n   \\[\n   P:=\\sum_{D_{c}\\in\\mathcal D^{c}}(c-1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contribute **at most one** point from each diagonal of \\(\\mathcal D^{c}\\); therefore a single sunny line can cover at most \\(|\\mathcal D^{c}|=n-t\\) of the uncovered points, and the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\,(n-t)\\) points.  \n   Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,(n-t)\\ge P . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k=n-t\\), (3) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-t)^{2}\\ge P . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Choosing the diagonals to make (4) easiest.**  \n   To satisfy (4) we may select the \\(t\\) *longest* diagonals, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their lengths are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\); their total contribution is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)=\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points in \\(S\\) is, using (1),\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (5)–(6) into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Re‑writing (4) with \\(k=n-t\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}\\ge\\frac{k^{2}+k}{2}\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k(k-1)\\ge0 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Consequently **no further restriction** on \\(k\\) follows from the counting argument: any \\(k\\) in this range is *a priori* admissible.\n\n5. **Existence constructions.**  \n   We now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Notation for the construction.*  \n   For a positive integer \\(m\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m}:\\;y=m\\,x+(m+1). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_{m}\\) is \\(m\\); therefore \\(L_{m}\\) is sunny for every \\(m\\ge 1\\) (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor any diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n   **(i)  \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny, and by (1) each point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one of these lines.\n\n   **(ii)  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Fix a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n   Solving the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x+y=c,\\\\[2pt]\n   y=m\\,x+(m+1)\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad (1\\le m\\le n)\n   \\]\n\n   gives the unique intersection point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(x_{m},y_{m}\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{c-m-1}{m+1},\\; \\frac{m(c-m-1)}{m+1}+m+1\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The numerator \\(c-m-1\\) is an integer. Moreover, for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) we can choose \\(m=c-a-1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\)). Substituting this \\(m\\) into (8) yields \\(x_{m}=a\\) and \\(y_{m}=c-a\\). Hence the point \\((a,c-a)\\in D_{c}\\cap S\\) lies on the line \\(L_{c-a-1}\\). As \\(a\\) runs through \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) we obtain **all** points of \\(D_{c}\\cap S\\). Since this holds for every diagonal, the family \\(\\{L_{m}\\}_{m=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(S\\).\n\n   **(iii)  General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   *Non‑sunny part.*  Take the \\(t\\) longest diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{s}=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage of the short diagonals.*  \n   The diagonals that are **not** in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n-t+1},\n   \\]\n\n   whose lengths are \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) respectively.  \n   Let \\(c\\) be one of these numbers (\\(2\\le c\\le n-t+1\\)).  \n   As in the proof of (ii), for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) we choose the line \\(L_{c-a-1}\\); note that \\(c-a-1\\) lies between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\), hence the corresponding line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{s}\\). Consequently every point \\((a,c-a)\\) of the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) is hit by a sunny line.  \n\n   *Disjointness.*  \n   No line of the form \\(L_{m}\\) (with \\(m\\ge 1\\)) is parallel to any diagonal, so the sunny lines never coincide with the chosen long diagonals; all lines are distinct.\n\n   Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\mathcal D\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal L_{s}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, all distinct, and it covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that a configuration can exist only if \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   The explicit constructions above demonstrate that every integer in this interval is indeed attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solut