y the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M(t)=\\max_{\\,\\text{choice of }t\\text{ non‑sunny lines}} \\bigl(\\text{sum of their individual coverages}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The maximal sum is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) longest possible non‑sunny lines, namely the diagonals \\(D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}\\). Their lengths are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   M(t)=\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The whole set \\(S\\) contains, by summing (1),\n\n   \\[\n   |S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines can each meet a given diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1); therefore a sunny line can contribute at most one new point from each diagonal that is **not** already covered by the non‑sunny lines. The number of such diagonals equals the number of diagonals omitted from the set used in (2), namely \\(n+1-2-(t-1)=n-t\\). Hence the total number of points that can be added by all sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,(n-t)=k^{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   To cover all points we need  \n\n   \\[\n   M(t)+k^{2}\\ge |S|.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(k=n-t\\) and using (2)–(3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}{2}+(n-t)^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\,(n-t)\\ge 0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is always true. However, equality must hold, because the left‑hand side cannot exceed \\(|S|\\) (the total number of points is fixed). Equality forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}=k\\,(n-t)=k^{2}=0,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(k=0\\). Thus **any** configuration that covers all points with exactly \\(n\\) lines must contain **no sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and by (1) every point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n5. **Concl