            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \nD_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\) we have (1) \\(D_{c}\\cap S=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1\\}\\) and \\(|D_{c}\\cap S|=c-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then for every diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) we have \\(|L\\cap D_{c}|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.* A sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal, hence it can intersect a diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).\n\n* Horizontal \\(y=b\\): the points of \\(S\\) on it are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus it contains \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points.\n* Vertical lines are analogous.\n* Diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S\\) (by (1)).\n\nHence the length of any non‑sunny line is at most \\(n\\), and the lengths of the diagonals are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nIf we are allowed to choose \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, the greatest possible number of distinct points of \\(S\\) they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) longest non‑sunny lines, i.e. the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2},\n\\]\n\nwhose lengths are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nM(t)=\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nis the maximal cardinality of the subset of \\(S\\) that can be covered by \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points still uncovered after the non‑sunny lines\n\nLet the configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; then \\(t=n-k\\).\n\nEach of the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines meets a given diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1). Consequently, on diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) at most \\(t\\) of its \\(c-1\\) points can already be covered. The number of points of \\(D_{c}\\cap S\\) that remain uncovered is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,(c-1)-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nSumming over all diagonals gives the total number of uncovered points:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(t)&=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{0,(c-1)-t\\}\\\\\n    &=\\sum_{j=t+1}^{n}(j-t) \\qquad (j=c-1)\\\\\n    &=\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  What sunny lines can add\n\nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point, so on a fixed diagonal it can contribute at most one of the still uncovered points. There are \\(n\\) diagonals, hence the total number of points that all \\(k\\) sunny lines together can add is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot (n-t)=k^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessary counting inequality\n\nThe whole set \\(S\\) contains, using (1),\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTo cover every point of \\(S\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\nM(t)+\\text{(points added by sunny lines)}\\ge |S|.\n\\]\n\nUsing the maximal possible contributions (2) and (4) yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2}+k^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qq