(0\\le t\\le n\\), the term \\(t(n-t)\\) is non‑negative and \\(\\frac{t(t-1)}{2}\\ge 0\\). Hence the left‑hand side of (6) is **exactly** \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) **iff** \\(t(n-t)=0\\).  \n\n   If \\(t\\) were strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) we would have \\(t(n-t)>0\\), which would make the left‑hand side **strictly larger** than \\(|S|\\). That would mean the \\(n\\) lines cover **more** points than actually exist in \\(S\\), an impossibility because the lines are required to be distinct and each point of \\(S\\) can be counted at most once in the covering condition. Therefore the only way for the inequality (6) to be satisfied without contradiction is\n   \\[\n   t(n-t)=0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t=0\\text{ or }t=n.\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(t=n\\) corresponds to \\(k=0\\); the case \\(t=0\\) would give \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. In the latter situation the bound (4) yields at most \\(n^{2}\\) covered points, whereas \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}<n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\); however the sunny lines cannot actually achieve the bound \\(n^{2}\\) because each sunny line can meet each diagonal at most once, and there are only \\(n\\) diagonals. A direct inspection shows that with only sunny lines many points of \\(S\\) remain uncovered (for instance \\((1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) and cannot be hit by a sunny line). Hence the case \\(t=0\\) is impossible. Consequently we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n,\\qquad k=0.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the family of the \\(n\\) diagonals\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and by (1) every point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) indeed exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces \\(k=0\\); the explicit construction shows that this value is attainable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \nD_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\) we have (1) \\(D_{c}\\cap S=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1\\}\\) and \\(|D_{c}\\cap S|=c-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then for every diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) we have \\(|L\\cap D_{c}|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.* A sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal, hence it can intersect a diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).\n\n* Horizontal \\(y=b\\): the points of \\(S\\) on it are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus it contains \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points.\n* Vertical lines are analogous.\n* Diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S\\) (by (1)).\n\nHence the length of any non‑sunny line is at most \\(n\\), and the lengths of the diagonals are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nIf we are allowed to choose \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, the greatest possible number of distinct points of \\(S\\) they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) longest non‑sunny lines, i.e. the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2},\n\\]\n\nwhose lengths are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nM(t)=\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nis the maximal cardinality of the subset of \\(S\\) that can be covered by \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points still uncovered after the non‑sunny lines\n\nLet the configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; then \\(t=n-k\\).\n\nEach of the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines meets a given diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1). Consequently, on diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) at most \\(t\\) of its \\(c-1\\) points can already be covered. The number of points of \\(D_{c}\\cap S\\) that remain uncovered is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,(c-1)-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nSumming over all diagonals gives the total number of uncovered points:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(t)&=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{0,(c-1)-t\\}\\\\\n    &=\\sum_{j=t+1}^{n}(j-t) \\qquad (j=c-1)\\\\\n    &=\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  What sunny lines can add\n\nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point, so on a fixed diagonal it can contribute at most one of the still uncovered points. There are \\(n\\) diagonals, hence the total number of points that all \\(k\\) sunny lines together can add is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot (n-t)=k^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessary counting inequality\n\nThe whole set \\(S\\) contains, using (1),\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTo cover every point of \\(S\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\nM(t)+\\text{(points added by sunny lines)}\\ge |S|.\n\\]\n\nUsing the maximal possible contributions (2) and (4) yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2}+k^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{with }k=n-t. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Solving the inequality\n\nInsert \\(k=n-t\\) into (6):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{t(2n-t+1)}{2}+(n-t)^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+t(n-t)-\\frac{t(t-1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\nt(n-t)\\ge\\frac{t(t-1)}{2}\\ge 0.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(0<t<n\\) then \\(t(n-t)>0\\) and the left‑hand side of (6) would be **strictly larger** than \\(|S|\\). That would mean the \\(n\\) lines together cover more points than actually exist in \\(S\\), which is impossible because each point of \\(S\\) can be counted at most once in the covering requirement. Hence the only possibilities are  \n\n\\[\nt=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad t=n.\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(t=0\\) (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nAll lines would be sunny. By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets each diagonal at most once, so at most \\(n\\) points of a given diagonal can be covered. Since diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains \\(n\\) points, covering it would already require all \\(n\\) sunny lines to be parallel to distinct points of that diagonal, which forces two of the sunny lines to be parallel—a contradiction to the distinctness requirement. Moreover, the point \\((1,1)\\) (on \\(D_{2}\\)) cannot be hit by any sunny line because any line through \\((1,1)\\) is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\), all of which are non‑sunny. Hence \\(t=0\\) is impossible.\n\n*Case \\(t=n\\) (\\(k=0\\)).*  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, which is admissible (see the construction below).\n\nThus the counting argument forces  \n\n\\[\nk=0.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n\\) distinct diagonals, none of which is sunny. By (1) each point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Therefore the configuration meets both requirements with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]"
