"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(k=1\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Impossibility of exactly one sunny line.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained exactly one sunny line, the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n   By a double\u2011counting argument on the points of \\(S_n\\) we show that at least two lines of the same forbidden direction are necessary; consequently at least two lines are non\u2011sunny, contradicting the assumption that only one line is sunny. Hence \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\n2. **Construction for all other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  An explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (with slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)) is exhibited; each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n   *\\(k\\ge 2\\).*  For a given \\(k\\) we first choose \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines: a suitable number of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines so that every point of \\(S_n\\) is already covered by these \\(n-k\\) lines.  Then we add any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (with slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) that do not coincide with the already chosen lines.  The covering property is preserved, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTogether these two parts give the exact set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.  \nConsequently the other \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each belongs to one of the three families (2).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\quad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  How many points can the non\u2011sunny lines cover?  \n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSumming over all horizontal lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|S_n\\cap\\bigcup_{y=c\\in\\mathcal H}\\bigr|\\le h\\,n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same bound holds for the vertical lines, giving at most \\(v n\\) points, and for the diagonal lines, giving at most \\(d n\\) points (a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the triangle in at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) lattice points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The single sunny line  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; therefore its intersection with the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is a line segment whose lattice points are a subset of a line of slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nA standard lattice\u2011point estimate (or a direct inspection) shows that such a line can contain **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Total number of covered points  \n\nAdding the contributions we obtain the following upper bound for the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le h n+ v n+ d n + n = (h+v+d+1)n = n\\cdot n = n^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (5).  Therefore a covering family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain exactly one sunny line.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\neq 1. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x-\\frac{i(i-1)}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(i\\) are distinct and none equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le b-a+1\\le n\\).  \nSet \\(i=b-a+1\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = i a-\\frac{i(i-1)}{2}\n   = (b-a+1)a-\\frac{(b-a+1)(b-a)}{2}=b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines, and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  The case \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n###### 3.2.1  Non\u2011sunny part  \n\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines we need).  \nChoose non\u2011negative integers  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\nv=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\nd=m-h-v. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d=m\\).\n\nSelect the following non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines  \n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h. \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n* Vertical lines  \n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v. \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n* Diagonal lines  \n  \\[\n  x+y=n+2-d,\\;x+y=n+3-d,\\dots ,x+y=n+1. \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines belong to the three forbidden families, are distinct, and there are exactly \\(m=n-k\\) of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n###### 3.2.2  Coverage by the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le h\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (9).\n\n*Otherwise, if \\(a\\le v\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (10).\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge (v+1)+(h+1)=v+h+2\\), the sum \\(c=a+b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nv+h+2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal family (11) contains **every** line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\) in this interval; consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n###### 3.2.3  Adding the sunny lines  \n\nWe still need \\(k\\) further lines to reach a total of \\(n\\).  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that are not parallel to the three forbidden directions and that do not coincide with any of the lines already selected.  \nFor concreteness we may take  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = \\Bigl(j+\\tfrac12\\Bigr)x,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(j+\\tfrac12\\) are all different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny and distinct from the horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines already used.\n\nThe final family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\{\\text{lines (9),(10),(11)}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, all lines are distinct, and, as shown above, the non\u2011sunny part already covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain exactly one sunny line (Section\u202f2).  \nConversely, we have exhibited explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) (Section\u202f3.1) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) (Section\u202f3.2).  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
