\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\,n+1-j),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are the points on the hypotenuse of the triangle \\(T_n\\); they are all distinct and satisfy \\(j+(n+1-j)=n+1\\).  \nThrough \\(P_j\\) draw a line of slope \\(-\\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\).  \nSince \\(-\\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(j\\) (the denominator is non\u2011zero and the fraction is never \\(-1\\)), each of these lines is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nThe line through \\(P_j\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(j-t,\\;n+1-j+t)\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the \\(j\\) points of the diagonal segment from \\((1,n)\\) to \\(P_j\\).  Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwhich proves (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) that together contain exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\cap T_n\n   \\]\n\n   the set of points of \\(T_n\\) already covered.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=T_n\\setminus S_k .\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) and (3),\n\n   \\[\n   |R_k|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n          =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Observe that every point of \\(R_k\\) lies on a unique vertical line \\(x=i\\) and on a unique diagonal line \\(x+y=j\\) with \\(i,j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=j\\mid j\\in J\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   I=\\{\\,i\\mid \\exists\\,b\\;(i,b)\\in R_k\\,\\},\\qquad\n   J=\\{\\,j\\mid \\exists\\,(a,b)\\in R_k\\;(a+b=j)\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covers \\(R_k\\).  By construction \\(|I|+|J|\\le n-k\\) (indeed each uncovered point contributes at most one new element to \\(I\\cup J\\), and there are at most \\(n-k\\) such contributions because the total number of points removed by the sunny lines is \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\ge k\\)).  \n\n3. **Completion.**  \n   Choose any set of exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); because \\(|\\mathcal F_k|\\ge n-k\\) this is possible.  \n   Denote these lines by \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_{\\,n-k}\\).  They are all non\u2011sunny (they belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\)) and together with the sunny lines they form a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,M_1,\\dots ,M_{\\,n-k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either in \\(S_k\\) (hence on some \\(L_i\\)) or in \\(R_k\\) (hence on some \\(M_j\\)).  Thus the first requirement of the problem is satisfied.  \n   The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is precisely \\(k\\).  \n\nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem asks for all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists.  \nSince we have exhibited a construction for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible.  \nConversely, the number of lines in the family is exactly \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  Hence no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
