  \n\n\\[\n(i-t,\\;n+1-i+t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,i-1,\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\) because their coordinates are positive and their sum is \\(n+1\\).  \nThus \\(L_i\\) contributes exactly \\(i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSumming over \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) gives (1). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Uncovered points are exactly the rightmost columns)\n\nWith the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) defined in Lemma 1, a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **not** on any \\(L_i\\) iff \\(a>k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(a\\) equals some index \\(i\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nWriting \\(t=n+1-a-b\\) (which is a non‑negative integer) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a-t,\\;n+1-a+t),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) is one of the points listed for the line \\(L_a\\) in Lemma 1 and therefore lies on a sunny line.  \n\nConversely, if \\(a>k\\) then the point cannot belong to any \\(L_i\\) because each \\(L_i\\) contains only points whose first coordinate is at most \\(i\\le k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from Lemma 1.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   By Lemma 2 the set of points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>k\\}\n        =\\bigcup_{j=k+1}^{n}\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(R_k\\) consists of the \\(n-k\\) vertical columns with abscissas \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n3. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) include the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines belong to the family \\(\\mathcal V\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n   They are distinct and together contain all points of \\(R_k\\).\n\n4. **The whole family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{M_{k+1},\\dots ,M_n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) then, as shown in Lemma 2, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n   - If \\(a>k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a=M_a\\).  \n\n   Hence every required point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a configuration satisfying both conditions exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists.  \nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these values are admissible.  \n\nConversely, the family must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
