            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) form a right‑angled triangular lattice with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nHence the three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to any line in (5)).  \n   Distinctness follows from the different intercepts \\(-i+1\\).\n\n2. **Points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line.**  \n   Substituting \\((x,y)=(i+t,1+t)\\) into (6) gives an identity, so every point of the form  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+t,\\;1+t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,n-i ,\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   Conversely, if \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(y=x-i+1\\) then \\(x-i=y-1\\ge0\\) and \\(x\\le n\\); writing \\(t=x-i\\) we obtain (7).  \n   Hence \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the \\(n-i+1\\) points listed in (7).\n\n3. **Coverage of points with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le k\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we can write \\(b=n+1-a-t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge0\\).  \n   Then \\((a,b)=(a+t,\\;1+t)\\) with \\(t=n+1-a-b\\), and (7) with \\(i=a\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\n4. **Remaining points.**  \n   The set of points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>k\\}\n        =\\bigcup_{j=k+1}^{n}\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_j\\) belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal V\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \n   The line \\(V_j\\) contains precisely the column \\(\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\}\\), i.e. the part of \\(R_k\\) with first coordinate \\(j\\).\n\n5. **The full collection of lines.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; exactly the first \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) then, as proved in step 3, \\((a,b)\\in S_a\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n   - If \\(a>k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\) by (9).  \n\n   Hence every point required by the problem lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness of the values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these values are attainable.  \n\nConversely, any admissible configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Thus no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
