.\n\n6. **Exhaustiveness**  \n   Because a configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. The construction above shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the three families of non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to any line in (4)).  \n   Distinctness follows because the intercept \\(-i+1\\) is different for different \\(i\\).\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x = j .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_j\\) belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal V\\) and is thus non\u2011sunny.  \n   The lines \\(V_j\\) are distinct because their \\(x\\)-coordinates differ.\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\underbrace{\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n                \\cup\\underbrace{\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}} .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Clearly \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\), we obtain \\(i\\le a\\le k\\); and because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) we have \\(i\\ge 1\\).  \nThus \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a - i + 1 = a - (a-b+1) + 1 = b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in S_i\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>k\\).*  \n\nThen by definition (6) the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains every point whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nSince the two cases exhaust all possibilities for \\(a\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). This proves the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Existence.*  \nThe construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore each such \\(k\\) is attainable.\n\n*Non\u2011existence of other values.*  \nIn any admissible configuration we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than \\(n\\) nor negative. Hence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised as the number of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions, and no other values are possible:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
