st one of the \\(n\\) lines, the remaining \\(|C_a|-1\\ge 1\\) points of \\(C_a\\) would require additional distinct lines.  \n\nThus the total number of lines needed would be at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{the line }L) \\;+\\; (|C_a|-1) \\;+\\; (n-1) \\;>\\; n,\n\\]\n\nwhere the last term \\(n-1\\) accounts for the other columns, each of which needs at least one line.  \nThis contradicts the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\nHence **no column can be covered by a sunny line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Determination of the number of sunny lines  \n\nSince every column \\(C_a\\) must be covered, and sunny lines are forbidden for this purpose, each column must be covered by a **vertical** line \\(x=a\\).  \nVertical lines belong to the family \\(\\{x=c\\}\\), which is precisely the set of lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; such lines are **not sunny** by definition.\n\nTherefore all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, and the number of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical (hence non‑sunny) lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (5).  \nThus the covering condition is satisfied, and \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  No other values of \\(k\\) are possible  \n\nThe argument of Section 3 shows that the presence of even a single sunny line forces the total number of lines to exceed \\(n\\).  \nHence any configuration satisfying the problem’s requirements must have **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe only non‑neg