cal, i.e. \\(V=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). In that case the right\u2011hand side equals \\(n\\).  \n\n   If any column is missing its vertical line, say \\(a\\notin V\\), then the contribution of that column to the sum is at least \\(2\\) (because \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(a\\le n-1\\)). Consequently the right\u2011hand side of (3) becomes **strictly larger than \\(n\\)** for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n   Therefore (3) can hold only when \\(V=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); that is, every column must be covered by its vertical line.\n\n6. **Consequences for sunny lines** \u2013  \n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are vertical, and vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; by definition they are **not sunny**. Hence the number of sunny lines is forced to be  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0 .\n   \\]\n\n7. **Existence** \u2013  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and clearly contains every point of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\)). Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n8. **Exhaustiveness** \u2013  \n   Because any configuration with at least one sunny line would leave some column without its vertical line, inequality (3) would be violated, and some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered. Hence no \\(k\\ge 1\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the given family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Columns  \n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=n+1-a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad |C_a|\\ge 2\\ \\text{for}\\ a\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  How many points of a column can a line contain?  \n\n*If a line is vertical* (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) it coincides with exactly one column and therefore can contain **all** points of that column.\n\n*If a line is not vertical* then it can be written as \\(y=mx+c\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the equation \\(x=a\\) together with \\(y=mx+c\\) yields a unique point \\((a,ma+c)\\); consequently a non\u2011vertical line meets the column \\(C_a\\) in **at most one** point.  \n\nThe same reasoning applies to horizontal lines (\\(m=0\\)) and to lines of slope \\(-1\\); they are also non\u2011vertical and therefore intersect each column in at most one point.\n\nHence we have the following fundamental fact.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(L\\) be any line that is **not** vertical. Then for every \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)  \n> \\[\n> |L\\cap C_a|\\le 1 .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Necessary presence of vertical lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=a\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nthe set of columns whose **vertical** line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nConsider a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\notin V\\).  \nSince its vertical line is absent, every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that meets \\(C_a\\) is non\u2011vertical; by Lemma\u202f1 each such line contributes at most one point of \\(C_a\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(|C_a|\\) distinct lines are required to cover all points of \\(C_a\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of lines needed satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn\\;\\ge\\;|V|+\\sum_{a\\notin V}|C_a|\n   =|V|+\\sum_{a\\notin V}(n+1-a).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Solving inequality (11)  \n\nFor each index \\(a\\) that is **removed** from the sum and placed into \\(|V|\\) the right\u2011hand side of (11) is reduced by  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-a)-1 = n-a \\ge 0 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the right\u2011hand side is **minimal** when the set \\(V\\) is as large as possible, i.e. when \\(V=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIn that case the sum disappears and (11) becomes \\(n\\ge n\\), which is true.\n\nAssume now that some column is missing its vertical line; pick any \\(a\\notin V\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\ge 2\\).  \nHence the contribution of this column to the sum in (11) is at least \\(2\\), while moving it to \\(V\\) would contribute only \\(1\\).  \nTherefore the right\u2011hand side of (11) would be **strictly larger than \\(n\\)**, contradicting the inequality.  \n\nThus (11) can hold only when  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every column is covered by its vertical line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Determination of the number of sunny lines  \n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are vertical (they are exactly the lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)).  \nVertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so by definition they are **not sunny**.  \nHence the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (15).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem, showing that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Impossibility of any \\(k\\ge 1\\)  \n\nIf a configuration contained at least one sunny line, that line would be non\u2011vertical, and by Lemma\u202f1 it could contribute at most one point to each column.  \nConsequently at least one column would lack its vertical line, contradicting the necessity proved in Section\u202f4.  \nHence no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"

