---------------------------------\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=a\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\qquad v=|V|,\\qquad t=n-v .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(v\\) is the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(t\\) the number of non\u2011vertical lines (sunny or horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nThe vertical lines cover exactly the points of the columns indexed by \\(V\\); their total number is  \n\n\\[\nP_V=\\sum_{a\\in V}(n+1-a).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points lie in the columns whose indices are not in \\(V\\):\n\\[\nR=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{a\\in V}C_a,\\qquad |R|=|T_n|-P_V .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim 1  \nFor every \\(a\\notin V\\) we must have \\(t\\ge n+1-a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\notin V\\).  \nSince its vertical line is absent, every point of \\(C_a\\) must be supplied by a non\u2011vertical line.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011vertical line can contribute **at most one** point to \\(C_a\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines are needed, i.e. \\(t\\ge n+1-a\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line; then \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\na_0=\\min\\{a\\mid a\\notin V\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(the smallest column without its vertical line).  \nBecause the indices \\(1,2,\\dots ,a_0-1\\) belong to \\(V\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nv\\ge a_0-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=n-v\\le n-a_0+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom Claim\u202f1 applied to the column \\(C_{a_0}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n+1-a_0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) forces equality throughout:\n\n\\[\nt=n+1-a_0,\\qquad v=a_0-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(|C_{a_0}|=n+1-a_0=t\\); the column \\(C_{a_0}\\) is completely covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011vertical lines, each line providing exactly one point of \\(C_{a_0}\\).\n\nBecause each of those \\(t\\) lines has already been used once in \\(C_{a_0}\\), Lemma\u202f1 implies that **none of them can supply any point in another column** (a non\u2011vertical line can meet a given column in at most one point).  \nHence all remaining columns \\(C_a\\) with \\(a>a_0\\) must be covered solely by vertical lines.  \nBut for \\(a>a_0\\) we have \\(a\\notin V\\) by definition of \\(a_0\\); therefore no vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present, and those columns remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nThus the assumption that a sunny (i.e. non\u2011vertical) line exists is impossible.  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are vertical, and vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny. Hence\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies the covering condition, and it contains no sunny line.  \n\nTherefore the only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
