g the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for each such slope take a line that passes through the point \\((1,1)\\).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines are sunny and intersect the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) in at least the point \\((1,1)\\).  \n\nNow we need to cover the remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nTake \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with constants  \n\n\\[\nc=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThese diagonal lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and together they cover every lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\) except possibly those lying on the first row or first column.  \nThe points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-k+1\\) lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+1\\); similarly the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-k+1\\) lie on the same diagonal lines.  \nThe only uncovered points are \\((1,1)\\) (already covered) and the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge n-k+2\\).  \nEach such point lies on one of the sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) because those lines have distinct slopes, and the set of slopes can be chosen so that the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) is among the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince there are exactly \\(k\\) different slopes available, we can assign a distinct sunny line to each of the at most \\(k\\) remaining points.  \n\nThus all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered, the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nA configuration with exactly one sunny line does **not** exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a configuration with precisely one sunny line \\(L\\) and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1, among the non\u2011sunny lines at least \\(n-2\\) must be diagonal.  \nHence at most one of the non\u2011sunny lines can be vertical or horizontal.  \nIf the remaining non\u2011sun