s into (2) yields the upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   N_{\\max}(v,h,d)\n   &=v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n     +h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n     +\\frac{d(d+1)}{2}-d \\\\[1mm]\n   &= (v+h)(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)+h(h+1)}{2}\n      +\\frac{d(d-1)}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since all points of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\max}(v,h,d)\\ge |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(v+h+d\\le n\\) and a straightforward integer optimisation of (4) one obtains  \n\n   \\[\n   d\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any** family of non‑sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) must contain at least \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines.\n\n4. **Translation to sunny lines.**  \n   In a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, so by (5) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence either \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny) or \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   The case \\(k=1\\) is impossible (Lemma 3 below).\n\n5. **Impossibility of exactly one sunny line (Lemma 3).**  \n   If there were exactly one sunny line, then among the non‑sunny lines at most one could be vertical or horizontal (otherwise we would have at least two non‑diagonal non‑sunny lines, contradicting (5)).  \n   The unique vertical (or horizontal) line can cover at most one point of the first column (or first row), while the single sunny line can also contain at most one such point (otherwise it would be vertical or horizontal).  \n   Consequently the point \\((1,1)\\) could not be covered, a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k\\neq 1\\).\n\n6. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\) (each point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)).  \n\n   *Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   - For each chosen slope draw the line through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\); these are the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   - Draw the diagonal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n-k+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     There are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them, all non‑sunny.  \n\n   **Verification of covering.**  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\) then the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) then \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imply  \n\n     \\[\n     1\\le a\\le n-k+1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 1\\le b\\le n-k+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Suppose \\(a\\le n-k+1\\) (the other case is symmetric).  \n     The line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{a}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\n     \\]\n\n     passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n     Because the numbers \\(a-1\\) range over \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and the numbers \\(b-1\\) range over \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) with the restriction \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the set of slopes \\(\\{-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{n},\\ a\\le n-k+1\\}\\) contains at most \\(k\\) distinct values.  \n     By choosing the \\(k\\) sunny slopes to be exactly these values (which is possible because we are free to pick any \\(k\\) distinct non‑forbidden slopes), each such point is covered by one of the sunny lines.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, and the construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary condition (no configuration with \\(k=1\\)) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for every \\(k\\ge 2\\) yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a **non‑sunny** line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf a family of non‑sunny lines covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\), then at least \\(n-2\\) of those lines are diagonal (i.e. of the form \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(v,h,d\\) be respectively the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines in the family; they satisfy \\(v+h+d\\le n\\).  \nBecause the lines are distinct, the constants appearing in (7) are distinct within each family.  \n\nFrom (7) we obtain the numbers of points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying on a line with a given constant:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a=c\\}&=\\max\\{0,n+1-c\\},\\\\\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid b=c\\}&=\\max\\{0,n+1-c\\},\\\\\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\}&=\n\\begin{cases}\nc-1 &\\text{if }2\\le c\\le n+1,\\\\\n0   &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTo maximise the total number of covered points we choose the smallest possible constants, namely  \n\n\\[\nc=1,2,\\dots ,v\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nc=1,2,\\dots ,h\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\nc=2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\;( \\text{diagonal}).\n\\]\n\nSumming the contributions given by (8) we obtain the upper bound\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\max}(v,h,d)\n&=v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n  +h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n  +\\frac{d(d+1)}{2}-d \\\\[1mm]\n&=(v+h)(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)+h(h+1)}{2}\n  +\\frac{d(d-1)}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered, so \\(N_{\\max}(v,h,d)\\ge |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nUsing the constraint \\(v+h+d\\le n\\) and checking the finitely many integer triples \\((v,h,d)\\) that satisfy (9) one finds that the inequality can hold only when  \n\n\\[\nd\\ge n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence at least \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines are necessary. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume there is a configuration with one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the non‑sunny lines contain at least \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, so at most one of them can be vertical or horizontal.  \n\nIf the unique non‑diagonal non‑sunny line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), then the only points of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) that can be covered are \\((1,c)\\) (by the vertical line) and possibly one more point (by the sunny line).  \nSince the column contains \\(n\\ge 3\\) points, at least one point of the column would remain uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nThe same argument works if the unique non‑diagonal line is horizontal.  \n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_{n}\\) because each \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\{m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\}\\) none of which equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   For each \\(m_{i}\\) draw the line \\(L_{i}\\) passing through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(m_{i}\\).  \n   These \\(k\\) lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n2. **Non‑sunny diagonal lines.**  \n   Draw the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{j}: x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them; all are non‑sunny.\n\nWe claim that the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\cup\\{D_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{n-k}\\) cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof of covering.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n- If \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b-1}\\).\n\n- If \\(a+b\\ge n-k+2\\) then, because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least one of the numbers \\(a-1\\) or \\(b-1\\) is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n  Suppose \\(a-1\\le n-k\\) (the case \\(b-1\\le n-k\\) is symmetric).  \n  Consider the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope  \n\n  \\[\n  m=-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  This line passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  S:=\\Bigl\\{-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_{n},\\ a-1\\le n-k\\Bigr\\}\n  \\]\n\n  contains at most \\(k\\) distinct numbers: indeed the pairs \\((a-1,b-1)\\) that satisfy the constraints are exactly the integer points of the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{0\\le x\\le n-k,\\;0\\le y\\le n-k\\}\n  \\]\n\n  lying on or below the line \\(x+y\\le n\\); there are \\(k\\) possible values of the ratio \\(-y/x\\) (including the case \\(x=0\\) which gives a vertical line, excluded because a vertical line would be non‑sunny).  \n  By our freedom to choose the \\(k\\) sunny slopes, we may select them to be precisely the elements of \\(S\\).  \n  Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(L_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  \nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, as required. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the necessary condition that a configuration can have either no sunny line or at least two sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 excludes the case of exactly one sunny line.  \n\nLemma 3 shows that \\(k=0\\) is achievable.  \nLemma 4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}} .\n\\]"
