n 2 shows that after each replacement every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered; the presence of the vertical line \\(V\\) guarantees coverage of all points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\) that might have been lost when a horizontal line is removed.\n\nHence after performing the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_{t_1},\\dots ,S_{t_k}\\)) and satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nSince the configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThus \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
