,\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n  which passes through \\((1,n)\\) (since \\(n = 1 + (n-1)\\)) and has slope \\(+1\\).  \n  Slope \\(+1\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n  Hence in this family  \n\n  \\[\n  k=1 .\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\nBecause at most one line of the family can be non‑horizontal (Lemma 1), it is impossible to have \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of coverage\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal L_0\\):*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\); it lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal L_1\\):*  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\subset\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) by construction (7).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of the two values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\):** the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (all horizontals) satisfies the conditions and contains no sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\):** the family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) with \\(\\ell\\) given by (7) satisfies the conditions and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\nBoth families consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
