            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(H_b\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\n\\qquad |P_b|=n+1-b .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, then it meets a given horizontal line in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑horizontal line has equation either \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq 0\\) or \\(x=c\\). Substituting \\(y=b\\) (the equation of a horizontal line) yields at most one solution for \\(x\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), the horizontals \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) must all belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose some \\(H_{b_0}\\) with \\(b_0\\le n-1\\) is missing from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nFrom (2) we have \\(|P_{b_0}|=n+1-b_0\\ge 2\\).  \nEach point of \\(P_{b_0}\\) must be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is not horizontal, because the only horizontal line containing points of \\(P_{b_0}\\) is \\(H_{b_0}\\) itself.  \nBy Lemma 1 a single non‑horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(P_{b_0}\\); therefore at least \\(|P_{b_0}|\\ge 2\\) distinct non‑horizontal lines would be required.  \nBut \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains the other \\(n-1\\) lines, which, by Lemma 2’s hypothesis, must be the remaining horizontals \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{b_0-1},H_{b_0+1},\\dots ,H_n\\). Hence there is room for at most one non‑horizontal line, a contradiction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Structure of admissible families\n\nLemma 2 forces the inclusion of the \\(n-1\\) horizontals  \n\n\\[\nH_1,\\;H_2,\\dots ,H_{n-1}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\) are already covered by (3).  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\) (since for \\(b=n\\) the set \\(P_n\\) consists solely of \\((1,n)\\)).  \n\nConsequently a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) can be only one of the following two types:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_0=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}}\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_1=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},\\ell\\}},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is any line through \\((1,n)\\) that is not one of the horizontals already listed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sunny lines\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions:\n\n* the \\(x\\)-axis (slope \\(0\\));\n* the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical, slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nAll horizontals \\(H_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.\n\n* In \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) there are no non‑horizontal lines, hence  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0 .\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n* In \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) the only candidate for a sunny line is \\(\\ell\\).  \n  Choose, for example,\n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\; y = x + (n-1) .\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n  This line passes through \\((1,n)\\) because \\(n = 1 + (n-1)\\). Its slope is \\(+1\\), which differs from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. Hence in this family  \n\n  \\[\n  k=1 .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\nBecause Lemma 2 forces the presence of the \\(n-1\\) horizontals, at most one line of the family can be non‑horizontal; consequently \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal L_0\\):*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); the point lies on \\(H_b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal L_1\\):*  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\subset\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) by construction (6).  \n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of the family in both cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of the two values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\):** take the family \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\).  \n* **\\(k=1\\):** take the family \\(\\mathcal L_1=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},\\ell\\}\\) with \\(\\ell\\) given by (6).  \n\nBoth families consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines and satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
