 k\\le n\\) we can exhibit a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n} (n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) the following three sets are subsets of \\(S\\):\n\n* the *row* \\(R_t=\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}\\);\n* the *column* \\(C_t=\\{(a,t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\}\\);\n* the *anti‑diagonal* \\(D_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By the definition of \\(S\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=t\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-t\\); similarly for a fixed \\(b=t\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=t+1\\) then \\(t+1\\le n+1\\) because \\(t\\le n\\); hence all points of the three sets satisfy the defining inequality of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) coincides with exactly one of the sets \\(R_t\\), \\(C_t\\) or \\(D_t\\) for some \\(t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) for some integer \\(b\\ge 1\\); intersecting \\(S\\) it consists of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-b\\), i.e. the row \\(R_{\\,b}\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) gives the column \\(C_{\\,a}\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); intersecting \\(S\\) it yields the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{\\,c-1}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nGiven any subset \\(U\\subseteq S\\) that is a union of whole rows, columns and anti‑diagonals, there exists a non‑sunny line covering all points of one of those rows, columns or anti‑diagonals that is not yet covered.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(U\\) contains at least one uncovered row, choose that row; by Lemma 2 the horizontal line containing it is non‑sunny and covers precisely that row.  \nThe same argument works for columns and anti‑diagonals. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Choose \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Enumerate the points of \\(S\\) arbitrarily as \\((p_1,q_1),\\dots,(p_{|S|},q_{|S|})\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) pick the line \\(L_i\\) that passes through the point \\((p_i,q_i)\\) and has slope \\(2\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) works).  \n   Because the slopes are all equal, the lines are distinct (they have different intercepts) and each is sunny.\n\n2. **Cover the remaining points with non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(U_0=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 each point of \\(U_0\\) lies on a unique row, column or anti‑diagonal.  \n   Apply Lemma 3 repeatedly: at step \\(j\\) (starting with \\(j=1\\)) choose a row, column or anti‑diagonal contained in the current uncovered set, draw the corresponding non‑sunny line, and delete its points from the uncovered set.  \n   Each step removes at least one previously uncovered point, and at most \\(n-k\\) steps are needed because there are exactly \\(n\\) rows, \\(n\\) columns and \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals, and we have already used at most \\(k\\) of them (the sunny lines never coincide with any of these three families).  \n\n   Consequently after at most \\(n-k\\) additional lines all points of \\(S\\) are covered.  \n\n3. **Count the lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)) and exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the ones obtained in step 2).  \n   All lines are distinct because the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) whereas the non‑sunny lines have one of those three slopes, and among the non‑sunny lines we never repeat a row, column or anti‑diagonal.\n\nThus for the given integer \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition obviously contains at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, and it may contain none (take all lines parallel to the coordinate axes or to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}\n\\]"
