(1\\le y\\le n+1-t\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the line \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(-(t-1)\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge 2\\), the slope is a negative integer \\(\\le -1\\) and is never \\(0\\) (horizontal), never undefined (vertical), and never \\(-1\\) (the forbidden diagonal). Hence \\(\\ell_t\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, i.e. it is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F =\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe case \\(a=n\\) cannot occur because then \\(b\\ge 1\\) would give \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\) with equality only when \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) is already on the line \\(x=n\\), which is not present in (2).  \nHowever \\((n,1)\\) belongs to the line \\(y=1\\) (see below), so every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(|S|=k\\).  \n\nDefine the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k =\\{y=1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=t\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus S\\,\\bigr\\}\\;\n\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_t\\mid t\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_t\\) with \\(t\\in S\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the remaining vertical lines).  \nAll lines are distinct because each vertical line has a different abscissa, each \\(\\ell_t\\) has a different slope, and \\(y=1\\) is neither vertical nor of the form \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the line \\(y=1\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)).\n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\notin S\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\in S\\) then, by Lemma 1, \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the required configuration exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*The problem of whether any further restrictions on \\(k\\) exist (i.e. whether some values in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are actually impossible) remains open.*"
